WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Zelda 2, The Adventure of Link, The Hyrule Fantasy continues.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:28.520
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.340
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:31.760
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:39.040
You guys will note that I had to say Zelda 2 and not The Legend of Zelda number 2.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.940
Yeah, it's like, you know, like Too Fast Too Furious or Fast Five.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:53.640
Yeah, you like understand that it's in The Legend of Zelda, but this game actually does not have The Legend of Zelda in its title.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.780
It's not a Legend of Zelda game.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.360
It's a Zelda game.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:03.860
Yeah. It's funny because in the manual it actually addresses The Legend of Zelda, which I don't even remember if the original did that.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.340
If anywhere the original says, here's The Legend of Zelda.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.340
But this says, here's The Legend of Zelda.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.220
Oh, I see what you're saying. Yeah, like the lore.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:20.240
Yeah. Like The Legend of Zelda, like the game was just, it was sort of like a, it was telling the legend of Zelda.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.500
And Zelda 2 is like, you know, this is what's really happening.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.840
It's not mythological, you know?

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:34.260
Right. But don't you think it's weird that Zelda 2 is about rescuing the original Zelda, like Zelda 1?

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:41.320
So I actually didn't know that until after I played the entire game and then read the story in the manual.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.980
And I was like, oh, it's a different Zelda.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:55.720
Wow. This is the one that I made sure I would read the story of because I imagined it was a pretty thick manual with a pretty sizable portion of it going towards story.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.200
So I did do my homework first.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.180
Yeah. Did it last.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.940
Yeah. You're bad at this.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:10.820
But it all, it all checks out, you know, it's the second Zelda game and it's the first Zelda princess who's apparently been sleeping for a very long time.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:18.320
And it's the same link, just different Zelda, but you don't meet the old Zelda that you rescued from the first game.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.380
Well, that's just because all, all girls, all girls are, they're named Zelda now.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.560
So any, any princess that you rescue is going to have to be Zelda.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.140
Well, I thought it was all girls born, born to the royal family.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.680
So like all the princesses, not like all in Hyrule.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.500
I thought it was everybody in the kingdom, but that makes more sense.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:45.720
Yeah. It's funny. Cause it is, I thought that it is worded like that at first, but if you, I think it does say like afterwards of the royal family.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.900
Okay. There's only two types of women in Hyrule.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:53.700
There's women named Zelda and women who hold pots over their heads.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.440
Uh, which is, which we'll get into, I guess.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:09.380
But you know, before we talk about the legend of Zelda to the adventure of link, we probably should just really fast remind everybody that we love the original legend of Zelda.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.400
And we have a two hour long episode.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.300
I think it even goes over two hours talking about how much we love that game.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.320
So we're really not going to do that here.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.960
We're not going to gush about the legend of Zelda.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.280
We're barely going to remind you why we like it.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.540
We're going to, we're going to try not to, at least we're probably going to bring up some examples.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:39.260
However, this is all about Zelda to the game that like, I think has lately gotten a resurgence, but for the most part, people see as the black sheep of the mainline Zelda games.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.160
Indeed. All, all of us have not played this game before.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.940
Am I, am I correct in saying that this is our first time playing this game?

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.680
Yes. This was my first time.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.900
Same. Yeah.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:58.560
So I think like with that, let's just get into the development of Zelda 2 to kind of explain what Nintendo was thinking and why the game changed so much.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.000
And then we can talk about our thoughts about the game from there.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.760
So this is almost a full two years after its initial release in Japan.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.900
Uh, the game came out in the Famicom disc system in 86.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:17.660
And finally, after like a, a way of trying to format it from the Famicom disc system back to the NES cartridge.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.540
And, uh, I think there was like a shortage of cartridges at the time for Nintendo.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.280
So they had to delay the game even further.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:29.900
It kind of just comes out around Christmas time for, uh, uh, December 1988.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.800
So we get the game now, but it's, it's actually been out for two years in Japan.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.620
And it's developed by a separate team from the Legend of Zelda.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.760
So even, uh, Miyamoto is just taking on a producer role.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.160
He's not necessarily making this game.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:54.620
Uh, key members, uh, of the Legend of Zelda's design team, such as, uh, Takashi Tezuka and Koji Kondo were not involved at all.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:05:02.200
So it's really just Miyamoto being the producer and, uh, Tadashi Sugiyama, uh, being the director on this one.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.320
Uh, development started with Mr.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:10.440
Miyamoto saying he wanted to make a side-scrolling action game that made use of up and down movements for attacks and defense.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.920
I love how that's how basic they start, you know?

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:20.880
Yeah. And it's also interesting because I didn't know that this whole genre shift came from Miyamoto.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:32.080
Like, I, I would have thought that, like, the basis of this whole change in the way that the entire game is played would have been because they passed it on to a different team.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.780
And they wanted to do something, like, their own with it.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.620
Uh, I guess my preconceptions of the game have, like, are kind of challenged by that story.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:50.860
Yeah, and even the idea, you know, uh, just, I want to make a side-scrolling action game of up and down movements.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:51.960
For attacks and defense.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.260
And that being, like, let's start there.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.600
And then, oh, that works for Zelda, so we'll just attach Zelda to it.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.360
I know that we've said that that's Miyamoto's design philosophy.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:07.260
But I can't imagine starting, like, with that vague of an idea and getting to a full video game.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.260
Well, it's actually hyper-focused of an idea.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.120
It's, it's just, I, I want this to be the mechanic.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.520
Uh, and then I guess you have to build every other mechanic around that.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:19.360
Which is also weird.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.460
Yeah, I wonder if it had become at, from the other direction, of, like, let's make a sequel to Legend of Zelda.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:32.280
If, if the thought of, of changing the main combat gameplay style or, or, or any of this stuff would have even come up.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:37.080
Joe, I think you're spot on because, uh, Shugiyama continues in his interview saying,

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:42.480
rather than being a continuation of the series, it started as a new sword and shield type of action game.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:49.040
We were experimenting while producing the game, so we didn't really have the first game systems in mind while developing it.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.800
As, as for it being unique within the series, we were searching for new ways to play.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.960
So, in some ways, you could actually say Zelda 2 is a spin-off, not a mainline game.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.780
Straight from Shugiyama himself.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.620
I guess that kind of explains the naming convention then.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.960
Right, rather than going full in on, on Legend of Zelda.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:10.680
Shugiyama continues here,

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:15.760
Games didn't have a ton of content at the time, so in order to have them played for as long as possible,

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.620
we felt like we couldn't make them easily clearable.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.000
We also did debugging, so we would play a game too much,

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.620
and the game would have to have a high difficulty that was interesting to us.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.500
One thing I remember is a call that we received from a customer at the time.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.040
He said he just couldn't beat the final boss.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.320
We talked with him and found out he was fully equipped,

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.100
so we had to tell him he could rely only on his own skills at that point.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:40.520
Pretty tough answer, right?

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.280
The person seemed to be playing on behalf of a child.

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:48.880
Sorry about that, Shugiyama replies 20 years later to the man that he told on the phone.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:51.440
You're on your own, buddy.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.760
Wait, someone called him, like, directly?

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.420
I guess they called Nintendo's Japanese hotline, you know, like, for help.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:01.340
And he was just, like, around the office to take the call?

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.980
You know, these aren't major, like, staffs at the time.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:11.240
Don't speak to your manager. Yeah, it's like there's only, like, 30 people working on a game,

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.680
like the Legend of Zelda 2, so I imagine, like,

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.100
I don't know if he was directly on the phone call,

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:17.640
but he probably heard about it, you know?

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:21.480
But it's not unheard of to think that, like, back during those times.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.800
Yeah, he's a liar. But back during those times, it wouldn't be unheard of that you could get a hold of.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:29.120
Like, remember, what was that, Gradius was the game where, like,

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.580
they went to that kid's school and gave him, like, a ton of money?

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:35.700
I just gave him money.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.860
That's right. That also kind of sounds made up, but that's beside the point.

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:45.180
And I think I have some Miyamoto stuff here, too.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.200
He says that Zelda 2 is a failure of a game.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:55.800
And he says, it was my idea, but the actual game developed by another team.

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:57.620
I mean, that's quick to shift the blame.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:03.760
I mean, if this is a situation where we want to assign blame and not praise.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.300
Right. No, I'm just saying Miyamoto is the one saying failure.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:14.000
Yeah. I wonder what, not that there are certainly flaws in this game,

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:20.720
but I wonder how much the backlash it got from fans affected his own opinion of it.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.680
Yeah, no, I think what wound up happening, Joe,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.440
is that they looked at Legend of Zelda 1,

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:30.660
and that was, like, a challenging game for them to make,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:34.540
but it had a huge payoff and reward that created a long-lasting franchise.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.800
And Zelda 2, Miyamoto says, actually, like, development went exactly as they expected.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.300
They pretty much took care of everything that they wanted to do on paper

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.940
and never really deviated beyond the paper plan.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:50.320
Like, they never really got creative in the making process.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.040
It was just they followed the documents.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:56.240
And so he says, all games I make usually get better in the development process

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.860
since good ideas keep coming.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:03.700
But Zelda 2 was sort of a failure because we just stuck with what we thought would be a good game.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.540
The interviewer then asks, so that's why the third game looked like the first one?

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.580
And Miyamoto answers, exactly.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.060
We actually see A Link to the Past as the real sequel to The Legend of Zelda.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:20.060
Zelda 2, more of a side story of what happened to Link after the events of Legend of Zelda 1.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.860
That's a really good insight into, I guess, game development at the time.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:30.540
Like, I never really thought of it as, like, a recipe.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:39.500
Like, you've got your recipe for the game and you just do what's written down for you, like, step by step.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:46.040
I always would have thought that even back then, like, a lot of the creative juices would be flowing

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:51.320
when you're actually coding it and testing it and playing around with, like,

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.040
what if I could do this instead and then going back and adjusting.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:59.740
Like, I never really thought of it as, like, step one, write down everything about the game,

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.800
and then step two, make it.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.000
Yeah, it's strange to think that, you know, especially because they were working with, like,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:16.900
assembly code back then, that you could, you know, that you could clearly write out a game

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:21.420
in documents first, you know, and then hand it over to the programmers and be like,

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.440
okay, here, make this, because Miyamoto knows next to nothing about programming.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.720
He is just a game designer and kind of comes up with the concepts behind these things.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:33.460
He's never been responsible, even in Super Mario Bros.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.540
1, that was Tezuka,

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:41.300
who was responsible for kind of, you know, the jump mechanics and figuring out that stuff.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.100
Miyamoto was just kind of the designer of the levels themselves.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:51.680
Yeah. And at that point, it's almost like your programmers are just, like, data entry.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:57.340
All right, so that's all for development, and that kind of tells two stories here.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:00.060
One, that this is not the original Legend of Zelda,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:04.400
and also that Nintendo's not quite happy with what they've put out here,

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:07.380
but we're going to stay neutral still, right, guys?

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.740
Of course. It's been a little difficult, it seems, but yeah.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.000
Okay, yeah, we're neutral, we're neutral.

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:17.180
So, here's a little thing, just to catch you guys up on the plot.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:22.560
Link is on a quest to save Princess Zelda, who is not the same princess from the first game,

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:25.320
but another member of the royal family by the same name,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.760
who was placed under a sleeping spell a long time ago.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.060
My question here, because it wasn't clear to me, maybe it's the answer to the manual,

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.860
is Ganon the one who put her to sleep?

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:37.980
I don't think so.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.220
No, I think it was just some wizard dude.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:44.080
So, Ganon really doesn't have much to do in Zelda 2.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:49.040
What I gathered from the manual story is that there's kind of just like two stories going on.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:53.300
One is that we learn that the blood of Link could restore Ganon.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.800
He's got that thing on his hand.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:57.760
Right, so that's like a stake.

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.260
Like, oh, if Link dies, Ganon can come back.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:08.860
And then, also, there's this other princess that's under a sleeping spell that he needs to find a Triforce

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:13.680
and unlock the temple with the Triforce, find it, and wake her up.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:19.160
I could be wrong on that, but that was how I interpreted it.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:21.620
There just happened to be two different things happening at once.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:26.180
Ganon is not necessarily related to the other thing,

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.440
other than the fact that if Link fails, Ganon will return.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.900
And the blood thing's important because there's like a cult of Ganon followers

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.760
who are like out to get you, right?

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.720
As a result of like they want to harvest your blood to Rizrek Ganon.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:41.860
Right, it's like very Voldemort.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:49.960
Yeah, you know, the title screen also has like a smaller version of, I guess, the manuals plot

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.520
where it does spell out a lot of the same things.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.160
And I don't know about you guys, but just from the title screen,

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:59.500
there was something like Star Wars-y about the title screen,

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:01.620
like the way that the music splashed on.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:02.920
The slow crawl.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.480
No, but even like even the design of the title, Zelda II, The Adventure of Link,

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:08.520
kind of looked like...

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:10.700
Yeah, like Episode II.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:12.200
Right, right, exactly.

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:14.560
The Zack of the Clones. Exactly.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:18.880
And so, I don't know, that probably worked to its advantage back in 1988.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:23.520
Yeah. But now it just feels overplayed, overdone, and not really fitting.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:30.240
I almost would have expected instead, you know, when you hold on a title screen too long,

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:37.940
it would then take you to a separate cutscene where you can see images of the story being played out or something.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:43.740
That's kind of what I expected instead, like a storybook version of what was happening instead of the scroll.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:47.940
Yeah, it is also like a starry night sky, right?

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:53.300
Which, to your point, doesn't really fit the game because there's never nighttime in this game as far as I'm aware.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.700
Nitpick! I mean, not that important, but just an observation.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:03.160
Well, technically, I mean, it could always just be night when you're in any of the caves, you know?

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:04.960
That's true. That must be when night falls.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:06.800
You only travel during the day.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:09.260
I think that's the important thing.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:10.500
That's true, yeah.

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:16.160
So the title screen is showing you what you're missing while you're in the caves, and it's like, don't spend your life in a cave.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:25.280
Yeah, that's what Miyamoto was trying to say after he had spent his whole childhood in caves to come up with The Legend of Zelda from his adventures in caves.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.280
He said, you know what, but that was a mistake, kids.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.040
Don't go in caves. Don't spend all your time in caves.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:31.920
Unless you have a candle, the power of the sun.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:35.440
We'll get to that later.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:38.700
Anyway, overview of the game, though.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:43.220
Just to explain, if you've never touched Zelda 2, you should definitely take a look at it.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:49.100
The Adventure of Link primarily features side-scrolling areas within a larger world map.

00:15:49.100 --> 00:16:00.560
The game also incorporates a combat system that is also side-scrolling and has more RPG elements, including an experience point system, which we will never see again in a Legend of Zelda game.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.460
Magic spells, which are sometimes brought back.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:10.200
And more interaction with non-player characters, a la, like for us, Castlevania 2.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.860
And another game we just played recently, too.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:14.920
I can't remember.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:17.640
I'm sure it wasn't important.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:20.880
But yeah, it's got side quests of all things.

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:23.140
Yeah, it's got side quests. It's got people you can talk to.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:28.840
We had the old men in Zelda 1, but I wouldn't consider that the same here.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:33.380
None of them in Zelda 1 had the same personality as Error.

