But it gives you like a moment to like square, usually, to like square off with that enemy,

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:19.400
and like it feels like a one-on-one little duel.

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:21.860
Sometimes there's more enemies, but usually it's like manageable.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:26.020
So I actually think that they handled the combat pretty well here,

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:30.880
if you're taking out the factor of like whether or not they should have kept it similar to the original.

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:39.720
Yeah, I think like combat in like palaces, which we'll get into more later,

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:44.540
I think that those are definitely more that whole like you can square off,

00:38:44.740 --> 00:38:47.600
and you kind of like focus on that one enemy.

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:52.460
Whereas I think with the random encounters, it does feel a little bit more spammed.

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:58.280
Which I know they're not all like that, but I think that the ones that I got into

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:04.860
was just a bunch of tiny mobs that you either were just going to try to avoid,

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:07.900
or you were just going to kill them all for experience.

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:17.580
That's sort of how I, like my dichotomy of like the battle design in those two instances,

00:39:19.540 --> 00:39:22.400
as for like random encounters in general,

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:28.780
I did appreciate that they would take away some of the frustration

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:36.900
that usually comes with random encounters by at least visualizing like where you would be,

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:41.280
or like where the enemies are so you can try and avoid them if you don't want to fight them.

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:42.640
It's hard.

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:45.600
It's hard to do that, but it's still a possibility.

00:39:47.440 --> 00:39:50.080
Yeah, I'd say those are my thoughts on random encounters.

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:54.260
Yeah, and another cool thing about the random encounters is that, you know,

00:39:54.440 --> 00:39:58.780
if you are on a certain kind of tile when you run into a random encounter,

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:04.580
the place where the fight takes place is relevant to the kind of tile you were on,

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:06.620
which I think is definitely a nice touch.

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:12.780
And also that you can choose, you know, if you're good at running away from your enemies,

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.440
you can choose to say, oh, I need more experience points.

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:20.720
I want to tackle like a harder kind of enemy in my random encounter.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:24.260
Or you can say like, no, I just want like some easy fodder.

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:26.060
I don't want to deal with this right now.

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:27.540
I just kind of get to the next town.

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:30.700
And you can choose to do those battles as well.

00:40:31.360 --> 00:40:37.320
Joe, you talked about the sword and shield aspect of the combat in the sense of like,

00:40:37.880 --> 00:40:43.560
you're using the shield to prevent attacks on you, and you're using the sword to strike back.

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:45.360
And yes, you can duck and you can jump.

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:52.800
So it sounds simple, but there's a lot of elements going on here because it's all live.

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:57.340
You got to remember, this is an action RPG in the sense that, you know,

00:40:57.460 --> 00:40:59.540
the enemy is constantly making decisions.

00:40:59.540 --> 00:41:00.940
You're constantly making decisions.

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:09.480
There's this samurai like cinema aspect to the fights, you know, like I know, Sean,

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:15.000
I know you were pushing against this, but pretend that it is just you versus one other enemy, right?

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:20.020
There is this aspect of like a game of chicken where you almost have to say.

00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:25.740
Yeah. Yeah, you have to say to yourself like, okay, he's going to attack right now,

00:41:25.780 --> 00:41:31.100
but I could block it with my shield, which I think is probably the best part of the combat, to be honest.

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:37.980
The fact that the shield actually works and that if you duck, you can protect your, you know, your kneecaps.

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:42.420
But if you are standing upright, that also like blocks fireballs and stuff like that.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:43.360
Like, I think that's great.

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:48.060
The problem here though, is if this is supposed to be samurai combat,

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:51.920
Link brought a dagger to a sword fight, okay?

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:55.220
That sword is way too short for this game.

00:41:55.760 --> 00:41:59.960
I can stand as close as I possibly think I should be to enemies,

00:41:59.980 --> 00:42:02.720
and I will without a doubt miss them.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:05.240
I feel like I need to be hugging these enemies.

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:10.400
I think Link is missing Simon's whip at this point, you know?

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:15.420
Like, it worked in Castlevania 2, a side-scrolling combat game, because he has a nice long whip,

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:17.480
or, you know, there's projectiles.

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:22.960
I know I'm going to get some pushback on this, but, you know, you got to tell me right now,

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:28.560
don't you wish the sword was just a little bit longer so that the combat would have handled better?

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:30.380
You're going to get no pushback from me.

00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:31.980
I agree entirely.

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:41.580
I also really like that whole chicken that you're talking about between, like,

00:42:41.620 --> 00:42:44.440
am I going to block high, block low, attack high, attack low?

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:52.360
So, and at times it did feel like a duel, like, against a mid-range enemy.

00:42:53.020 --> 00:42:59.140
But at other times, in conjunction with just how short your sword is,

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:03.120
I felt like I wasn't even, I couldn't figure out some of these enemies,

00:43:03.160 --> 00:43:06.360
because it felt random whether I was hitting or not.

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:15.040
So I know that they have shields themselves, but it just felt like I would randomly clink,

00:43:15.060 --> 00:43:20.960
but, like, clink against their armor, and then the next hit that seemed to happen

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:25.320
in the same sprite sequence, that one would hit.

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:34.100
So I wasn't really fully understanding that mechanic, as opposed to,

00:43:34.180 --> 00:43:36.500
I imagine maybe you guys could figure it out.

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:43.940
Well, there's an enemy in the first palace that made me look up something on game FAQs,

00:43:43.980 --> 00:43:48.300
because I just couldn't wrap my head around, like, why I wasn't doing any damage to him.

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:53.180
And they suggested figuring out how to, like, jump and then strike.

00:43:53.180 --> 00:43:56.700
So you kind of hit them above their shield, like you're hitting them on their head.

00:43:56.860 --> 00:44:03.360
Interesting. And I don't know if that is just a glitch, but it did help for, like, a combat point of view,

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:05.080
of, like, having to jump and strike.

00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:08.400
Like, I do think that that's a valid thing and not just an exploit.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:11.500
But that's what I wound up doing for certain enemies.

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:15.240
And then I think there were skeleton enemies sometimes that, you know,

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:17.340
you kind of had to strike them in the legs.

00:44:17.520 --> 00:44:19.560
Yeah, the skeletons I had no problem with.

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:23.260
The ones that, I think it's the same enemy you were talking about.

00:44:23.480 --> 00:44:27.080
I know that they all have names, but they're all bullshit names.

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:31.500
But it's the one that would, like, he would move his shield up and down,

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:36.180
and then, like, he'd sort of give you a little telegraph of whether he was going to strike high or low.

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:38.100
And he was orange.

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:40.260
I think that's the same one you're talking about.

00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:43.820
Yeah, absolutely. I feel like there ended up being, like, multiple variations.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.240
I mean, there weren't variations of the same enemy,

00:44:45.320 --> 00:44:49.060
but variations of the same enemy behavior as the game went on.

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:50.460
That was what you're describing.

00:44:51.200 --> 00:44:52.140
Next with boomerangs.

00:44:52.680 --> 00:44:54.160
Yeah. Oh, yeah. That's huge.

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:56.420
I feel like they weren't actually quite as bad, though.

00:44:56.660 --> 00:45:03.280
No. But the funny thing is I actually am just watching footage.

00:45:03.420 --> 00:45:04.680
I usually just watch footage while we record.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:09.580
And as I'm watching it, I'm like, oh, yeah, like, that is a ridiculously short sword.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:12.700
That thought actually never crossed my mind while I was playing, though.

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:16.060
I did have frustrations with the same kinds of enemies,

00:45:17.620 --> 00:45:19.300
but I had, like, mixed feelings on them a little bit.

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:21.720
But now that you've mentioned it, Mike,

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:26.400
I do think that, like, having, like, a little bit of a,

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:28.480
I don't know if you'd call it a hitbox if you're the one striking,

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:32.200
but a little bit of a bigger, like, sword, I guess,

00:45:33.320 --> 00:45:35.280
would have maybe remedied some of this.

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:38.260
Because I found myself doing little tricks like that, too.

00:45:38.360 --> 00:45:40.100
I found myself jumping a lot for those enemies.

00:45:40.280 --> 00:45:43.580
And I found myself doing weird things like running away from the enemy

00:45:43.580 --> 00:45:45.240
and then quickly turning around and stabbing low.

00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:46.660
And, like, that just worked.

00:45:46.780 --> 00:45:48.740
But, like, thinking about it, there was really no reason

00:45:48.740 --> 00:45:49.800
that should have worked that way.

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:54.080
I just learned it. So, like, maybe that's a little flaw.

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:57.200
But to me, it still doesn't take away too much

00:45:57.200 --> 00:46:00.760
from how much I actually did enjoy the combat and how fluid it is.

00:46:01.620 --> 00:46:04.760
And I don't think a longer sword would have made the game, like,

00:46:04.980 --> 00:46:06.240
significantly easier.

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:10.260
I think it would have just helped with some of the annoying aspects

00:46:10.260 --> 00:46:12.160
of the combat system.

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:16.300
Because you can get better at fighting with the shorter sword.

00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:19.300
You just need to realize that you have to get really close to enemies

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:23.440
and, you know, maybe sometimes cast a shield magic spell on yourself

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:25.640
if you anticipate taking a lot of damage.

00:46:26.720 --> 00:46:29.520
But because the sword, you know, is what it is,

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:33.500
and you only learn two optional attacks late into the game

00:46:33.500 --> 00:46:36.120
that I frankly think Link should have just always had.

00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:38.620
I know that probably would have made him powerful.

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:41.620
Down and up. Yeah, the down and up strikes.

00:46:41.840 --> 00:46:45.560
Like, I think those probably would have made him pretty op at the beginning.

00:46:45.560 --> 00:46:48.720
But this is the same guy who beat Ganon in the first game.

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:51.160
So he should be pretty strong to start.

00:46:51.980 --> 00:46:54.800
You know, I'm aware of, like, when you have full health,

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:56.540
the projectile sword thing.

00:46:56.660 --> 00:46:58.900
Like, that's awesome. It's so easy to lose that, though.

00:46:59.600 --> 00:47:01.600
Yeah. How often in this game do you have full health?

00:47:01.740 --> 00:47:03.400
Not very. I didn't have it very long.

00:47:03.700 --> 00:47:07.740
I also felt like maybe the sword thing wasn't quite as important

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:10.080
as it was in the first game.

00:47:10.100 --> 00:47:13.400
Because there are a lot of enemies that the projectile sword doesn't affect anyways.

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:16.880
So it was nice to have, but it didn't, like, bother me too much

00:47:16.880 --> 00:47:19.320
that my health wasn't always full because I was like, well, I'd probably be...

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:21.680
But you can at least, like, stagger these guys, I think,

00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:24.180
with the projectile sword.

00:47:24.460 --> 00:47:26.560
But I could be wrong there.

00:47:27.040 --> 00:47:28.560
Yeah, like, freeze them up, you're saying.

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:31.240
Yeah. I didn't notice that.

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:32.260
That could be true.

00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:33.240
I didn't notice it.

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:35.660
But that's also probably because I was almost never at full health.

00:47:35.780 --> 00:47:42.520
Yeah. Yeah, but I think, like, there's a system here in the combat

00:47:42.520 --> 00:47:46.640
that because it's not hearts, right?

00:47:46.780 --> 00:47:48.380
Because in the original Legend of Zelda,

00:47:48.380 --> 00:47:51.280
you had hearts that you kept collecting heart containers for

00:47:51.280 --> 00:47:54.240
and you could have, like, a crazy number of hearts at the end of the game

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:55.060
if you collected them all.

00:47:55.180 --> 00:47:57.640
Or you could just try and stick with, like, your three hearts.

00:47:57.920 --> 00:48:02.040
I think in this game, there's the live system.

00:48:02.380 --> 00:48:06.700
You know, the fact that you can, like, have extra lives as Link,

00:48:06.740 --> 00:48:09.320
which is something you'll never see again in the Legend of Zelda.

00:48:10.080 --> 00:48:13.760
You know, that's, like, my best guess is I don't see another way

00:48:13.760 --> 00:48:16.680
they'll do that again. This is definitely, like, Super Mario Brothers inspired.

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:22.240
But it makes the combat kind of forgiving.

00:48:22.240 --> 00:48:24.340
Like, you're okay to take some risks.

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:28.480
I think there's certain moments where I would have been like,

00:48:28.700 --> 00:48:29.880
oh, I just took some damage.

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:33.080
You know, like, I'm not going to make it through this fight.

00:48:33.100 --> 00:48:35.120
But when you have an extra life or something, you're like,

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:36.940
eh, whatever, I'll keep going for it.

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:40.040
You know, if I thought I was going to get a game over screen as a result,

00:48:40.240 --> 00:48:43.080
I'd be like, you know, I'd probably run away from the battle.

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:48.720
Now, could you remind me what happens when you lose a life?

00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:52.060
When you lose a life, I'm trying to think on the overworld.

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:53.720
When you lose a life, definitely in a temple,

00:48:54.040 --> 00:48:56.520
you end up back in that temple until you get a game over.

00:48:57.020 --> 00:48:59.440
I think when you just lose a life, you end up, yeah.

00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:03.000
Then once you get a game over, you end up back at the beginning of the game.

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:04.840
But, like, everything you've unlocked is unlocked.

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:08.380
So they did a nice little thing of having shortcuts you could unlock.

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:12.760
So then you don't, you know, or giving you tools that then mean you don't have to re-traverse everything.

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:15.900
You can now smash a rock that was breaking your way to get back to where you were.

00:49:16.120 --> 00:49:18.720
So I think it's like a good balance in punishment.

00:49:19.120 --> 00:49:25.940
Yeah. I know that there is that discrepancy between the majority of the game,

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:31.300
where the game over sends you back to Zelda's sleep spot.

00:49:32.180 --> 00:49:37.140
But then if you were to die or game over in the final dungeon,

00:49:37.460 --> 00:49:39.600
then it just brings you back to the beginning of the final dungeon,

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:44.900
which I know confused a lot of people, at least back in the day.

00:49:44.900 --> 00:49:53.440
But I think that with today's views on difficulty, this is more than reasonable.

00:49:53.720 --> 00:49:58.600
And you guys didn't talk too much about the shield, the shielding part of this.

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:05.140
Did you think about the shield a lot as your combat, or were you primarily on the offense?

00:50:05.140 --> 00:50:11.360
Oh, no, I was definitely thinking shield first, especially when you've got projectile enemies

00:50:12.440 --> 00:50:16.240
that are one way or the other going to hit your head or your knees.

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:18.720
Yeah, I think you have to think shield.

00:50:18.940 --> 00:50:24.520
I mean, I'm sure you could play it another way, but there's just so many things coming at you,

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:28.180
especially, you know, you get the boomerang guys where you got to, like, fight them,

00:50:28.300 --> 00:50:31.360
but also worry about the boomerangs that are behind you that are coming back.

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:34.000
It feels kind of cool.

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:36.960
It's hard, but it feels kind of badass when you pull off, like,

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:40.600
a little, like, three little dinks against your shield at once.

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:43.000
Yeah, yeah, with a lot of things.

00:50:43.080 --> 00:50:46.900
Or even some of those enemies we were talking about where they kind of mimic your movements with the shield,

00:50:47.040 --> 00:50:51.640
they throw daggers high and low, and it's kind of cool to be getting closer and closer to them,

00:50:51.720 --> 00:50:56.000
like, ducking to block low daggers, jumping or standing up to block high daggers,

00:50:56.040 --> 00:51:00.180
and, like, making progress getting closer to that guy without getting hit.

00:51:27.640 --> 00:51:30.280
I think it's interesting how,

00:51:30.280 --> 00:51:35.920
in the combat design of the game, you know, it did seem like they wanted you to take your time,

00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:39.620
you know, like, use the shield to your advantage, strike only when necessary.

00:51:40.100 --> 00:51:43.120
That's going to make for a very slow-paced kind of game,

00:51:43.120 --> 00:51:48.020
but I don't think that the game gives you a ton of enemies at any given time, you know?

00:51:48.160 --> 00:51:50.100
We talked about briefly in the palaces,

00:51:50.520 --> 00:51:53.560
you'll see usually, like, an enemy at a time,

00:51:53.580 --> 00:51:56.000
or maybe, like, smaller enemies, a few of them,

00:51:56.000 --> 00:51:57.920
but for the most part, like, it's spread out.

00:51:58.060 --> 00:52:01.220
And then the overworld encounters, the random ones,

00:52:01.500 --> 00:52:04.240
those ones, most of the time, they give them to you spread out,

00:52:04.320 --> 00:52:05.580
but you also have, like, a choice there.

00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:08.240
You can just flee battle, too, if you don't want to deal with it.

00:52:08.240 --> 00:52:13.560
So, they did slow down the gameplay combat overall in this game,

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:19.840
but where that doesn't quite work out for me is the introduction of the experience system,

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:23.920
you know? Zelda, one, didn't have an experience system at all,

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:27.020
and I don't think any Zelda has had an experience system since,

00:52:27.080 --> 00:52:28.960
and I don't really think they need experience systems,

00:52:28.960 --> 00:52:33.300
because the whole point of, like, your journey is to, you know,

00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:39.820
find new power-ups and find, and, you know, get new skills and stuff as you traverse.

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:45.700
So, the idea of, like, killing a certain amount of enemies unlocks a certain thing is strange here.

00:52:45.940 --> 00:52:49.280
You know, you fight to press on and continue the journey,

00:52:49.280 --> 00:52:51.780
not grind for stronger attack power.

00:52:51.780 --> 00:52:58.740
And so, when you mix, like, slow combat with the idea of grinding enemies that give you,

00:52:59.240 --> 00:53:02.080
you know, two to five experience every time you kill them,

00:53:02.180 --> 00:53:09.340
and you need, towards the late game, you need thousands to continue to experience your,

00:53:09.620 --> 00:53:13.980
you know, to gain your experience points to get level-ups in your health and magic and attack power,

00:53:14.140 --> 00:53:17.700
I think that's where the game all of a sudden gets weird for me, you know?

00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:19.780
All of a sudden, you have to start grinding.

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:21.420
I kind of disagree there.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:26.820
I actually, again, it might come from me just not playing really many other Zelda games,

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:29.080
but I really enjoyed the experience system.

00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:30.720
I think it could have been improved.

00:53:30.720 --> 00:53:34.560
I think it is weird in the late game that their enemies are still giving you two points of experience,

00:53:34.560 --> 00:53:37.440
but there are plenty of enemies that are also giving you, like, 100 points.

00:53:39.760 --> 00:53:42.720
But I found it very satisfying to level up,

00:53:42.740 --> 00:53:48.620
and it's one of the first times I feel like we've leveled up in an action-type game like this

00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:54.220
that you really feel the progress of, like, oh, this guy used to take me five hits,

00:53:54.280 --> 00:53:55.440
now he takes me two hits to kill.

00:53:55.540 --> 00:53:56.460
Like, I feel stronger.

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:59.460
And I also never feel like I needed to grind.

00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:04.720
I felt like there were times that I wanted to grind because I was in a temple,

00:54:04.760 --> 00:54:09.580
and at the end of a temple, you get whatever is left over to get you to that next level.

00:54:09.700 --> 00:54:13.500
So I always wanted to be, like, at the beginning of a, you know,

00:54:13.540 --> 00:54:17.200
I wanted to be at zero experience towards the next level so I can get a full level out of it.

00:54:17.320 --> 00:54:18.260
But that wasn't necessary.

00:54:18.280 --> 00:54:19.740
I always felt like I naturally got enough.

00:54:19.840 --> 00:54:21.140
Like, if I were to play this game without grinding,

00:54:21.140 --> 00:54:23.380
I still think I would have been strong enough at the end to beat the game.

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:29.560
Yeah, I think that it was unobtrusive enough that while I didn't ask for it,

00:54:29.660 --> 00:54:31.080
I didn't have an issue with it.

00:54:31.280 --> 00:54:35.560
Like, it was just basically you defeat an enemy, you cross that threshold,

00:54:35.620 --> 00:54:40.280
and the game is just like, you want to be stronger, or do you want to be more magic-y,

00:54:40.440 --> 00:54:42.580
or do you want to take more hits?

00:54:43.120 --> 00:54:45.880
And I was like, oh, sure, I'll take more hits.

00:54:45.880 --> 00:54:47.000
I appreciate that.

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:48.680
And then I just continued on my way.

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:56.500
So I was never grinding, and I also didn't really feel any different after that upgrade.

00:54:56.740 --> 00:55:01.160
So while I don't know how necessary it was,

00:55:01.440 --> 00:55:07.000
I also, it didn't turn into an impediment on the game, or my enjoyment of the game.

00:55:07.180 --> 00:55:10.600
I feel like we are, like, a nice range of opinions on this.

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:14.920
Because I feel like, Mike, you didn't care for it.

00:55:14.920 --> 00:55:19.100
It sounds like, Sean, you're kind of indifferent, but you know, you didn't dislike it.

00:55:19.260 --> 00:55:22.960
And I actually kind of really, I think I would have liked this game a lot less if it weren't for,

00:55:23.040 --> 00:55:24.860
not a lot less, I still would really like this game.

00:55:25.160 --> 00:55:31.460
But this was, like, a significant, like, plus for me, having the grinding,

00:55:31.620 --> 00:55:32.820
because I feel like they did it pretty well.

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:35.100
The grinding, I mean the experience, not the grinding.

00:55:35.720 --> 00:55:37.240
Yeah, no, I know what you meant, too.

00:55:37.320 --> 00:55:40.200
And I'm not saying that you have to grind to beat the game.

00:55:40.360 --> 00:55:44.920
I'm saying there's, like, this weird at-odds system with itself of saying, you know,

00:55:44.980 --> 00:55:48.020
combat is meaningful and should be taken slow,

00:55:48.540 --> 00:55:50.940
but we're not giving you a ton of experience,

00:55:50.940 --> 00:55:53.120
so you're going to have to do this often.

00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:56.700
It just, I felt like, for a character like Link,

00:55:58.040 --> 00:56:01.940
he should just get stronger as he continues on his journey,

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:06.060
not, like, through grinding various enemies and stuff like that.

00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:09.180
Also, the fact that the experience system never changes,

00:56:09.180 --> 00:56:11.800
and this is something that I'm spoiled from from later games, you know,

00:56:11.840 --> 00:56:14.060
like, who knows what experience systems were like,

00:56:14.560 --> 00:56:18.360
although I'm pretty sure by this point Dragon Quest had come out in Japan.

00:56:19.080 --> 00:56:23.100
You know, the experience system just offers you the same three choices every single time,

00:56:23.660 --> 00:56:26.320
and, you know, it's great, better than one choice,

00:56:26.380 --> 00:56:30.860
but I felt like, you know, a real level-up system would have offered you,

00:56:30.860 --> 00:56:36.300
like, some cool new attacks or, like, you know, bombs or something,

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:40.700
like, something interesting that you could then, like, have in your arsenal for leveling up,

00:56:40.980 --> 00:56:43.740
not just, okay, now you're a little bit stronger,

00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:46.860
or now you have a little more magic, you know, to you.

00:56:47.920 --> 00:56:52.240
You know, a system needs to be in place for these experience systems,

00:56:52.280 --> 00:56:56.140
so it's just, it's small, and it's small enough that it doesn't bother me so much, you know?

00:56:56.140 --> 00:56:58.600
It's just a small thing that I didn't like,

00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:00.960
but it does, you know, it makes for,

00:57:01.620 --> 00:57:06.900
it makes for healthy reasons for the random encounters to exist in the first place, right?

00:57:06.920 --> 00:57:09.260
Like, if an experience system is designed,

00:57:09.540 --> 00:57:12.280
at least there are random encounters so you can pursue

00:57:12.280 --> 00:57:15.280
to either do a very low-level run,

00:57:15.280 --> 00:57:19.880
or to grind the heck out of it and be super strong by the fourth palace, you know?

00:57:19.880 --> 00:57:25.540
I think, from that perspective, it almost seems like the only reason that each of them exists

00:57:25.540 --> 00:57:27.720
is for each other, and if you removed both,

00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:32.540
then maybe the game wouldn't be, like, any more, any worse.

00:57:33.840 --> 00:57:37.480
But I do think, just to step back a little bit,

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:44.700
I do think that the random encounters make the world feel a little bit riskier,

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:50.220
because otherwise it's just a glorified map that you have to navigate.

00:57:51.980 --> 00:57:57.760
So I think that the random encounters help both justify the experience system

00:57:57.760 --> 00:58:00.140
and justify the map.

00:58:00.320 --> 00:58:04.880
But, again, I didn't really have too big of an issue with the leveling up,

00:58:04.900 --> 00:58:07.920
because I didn't really even notice much about leveling up.

00:58:09.080 --> 00:58:12.080
So I want to, maybe this is a little bit of a side note here,

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:15.400
but I want to ask a question, and based on your answer, I might feel very stupid.

00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:18.760
But something both of you said made me think,

00:58:19.280 --> 00:58:23.800
were you guys able to choose what power-up you got at the end once you leveled up?

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:26.580
Because I didn't think you could, and it just went...

00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:28.080
It depends on how, you can cancel.

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:30.400
So this is the thing, you can cancel and wait.

00:58:30.920 --> 00:58:35.500
And because usually, after the first one, they all cost different values,

00:58:35.960 --> 00:58:39.700
so you can save up some more, and then the next time you hit that threshold,

00:58:40.000 --> 00:58:41.820
they'll say, like, okay, here's another choice.

00:58:41.820 --> 00:58:45.800
Do you want one of these? I'll be honest, I didn't even glance at the values.

00:58:45.880 --> 00:58:48.580
I was just like, level up, it's telling me attack, so I'm going to get attack.

00:58:48.680 --> 00:58:52.120
And it just works out that each level up, you get the next one down the line.

00:58:52.140 --> 00:58:54.420
So I still got evenly leveled up throughout the game.

00:58:54.520 --> 00:58:56.520
And it was only on the last one that it let me choose all three,

00:58:56.620 --> 00:59:00.600
I guess, because now I'm realizing, because I had enough experience to afford three.

00:59:02.320 --> 00:59:03.520
Wow, I didn't even notice that.

00:59:03.520 --> 00:59:06.820
But it worked out, because I leveled up pretty evenly across the board.

00:59:07.380 --> 00:59:11.160
Yeah, you can choose, and that's another cool point to the experience system, right,

00:59:11.200 --> 00:59:12.280
is that you can choose.

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:14.100
And I liked it without even knowing that.

00:59:14.880 --> 00:59:17.380
That's funny, but also, you know, Joe, it's interesting,

00:59:17.500 --> 00:59:19.600
because in the Japanese version of the game,

00:59:19.780 --> 00:59:22.800
they actually didn't make it so that they are valued differently.

00:59:22.800 --> 00:59:26.380
It was always the same cost for all three of them,

00:59:26.380 --> 00:59:30.200
so it made for much different loadouts, I suppose.

00:59:30.880 --> 00:59:35.300
Of, you know, you could be very strong a lot faster,

00:59:35.300 --> 00:59:38.000
because it required less points overall.

00:59:39.060 --> 00:59:44.540
Huh. Yeah, I don't really know what that would change.

00:59:44.540 --> 00:59:47.940
I wonder how different it would feel,

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:54.340
because, like, aside, in the American version,

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:55.940
where it's sort of, like, on a curve,

00:59:56.980 --> 01:00:00.100
I guess because I didn't really notice it too much that way,

01:00:00.120 --> 01:00:02.080
maybe I wouldn't notice it that much the other way either.

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:05.100
Yeah, I think you'd just level up faster is all,

01:00:05.140 --> 01:00:06.640
and you'd be a little bit stronger,

01:00:06.700 --> 01:00:08.100
but if you didn't notice it, it doesn't matter.

01:00:08.220 --> 01:00:10.620
Yeah. I also made one small note that the experience system

01:00:10.620 --> 01:00:12.180
is very similar to Rambo.

01:00:12.400 --> 01:00:13.820
Do you guys remember playing Rambo?

01:00:14.520 --> 01:00:18.880
Joe, you said that this game makes you feel like

01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:20.760
your attacks are doing more damage

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:22.700
in the sense of something that used to take five strikes,

01:00:22.700 --> 01:00:26.600
takes two. I don't know if you remember Rambo or not,

01:00:26.680 --> 01:00:28.700
but it did work on a similar system.

01:00:28.920 --> 01:00:31.820
You killed things, and you got stronger as a result,

01:00:31.880 --> 01:00:33.820
and things were supposed to take less kill.

01:00:34.080 --> 01:00:35.580
Less to kill. Less kill.

01:00:37.560 --> 01:00:40.400
It's funny, because I thought, like,

01:00:40.400 --> 01:00:41.780
oh, is this kind of like Rambo?

01:00:41.920 --> 01:00:42.800
But then I couldn't remember.

01:00:43.080 --> 01:00:44.320
Was Rambo anything like this?

01:00:44.440 --> 01:00:46.360
I was like, I actually didn't remember.

01:00:46.480 --> 01:00:48.160
It was like, I don't know. Why am I thinking Rambo?

01:00:48.240 --> 01:00:49.240
What the heck was that game?

01:00:49.260 --> 01:00:53.320
I don't remember. So I guess there was something similar about it.

01:00:53.680 --> 01:00:56.220
It was like Zelda 2, but with cut scenes

01:00:56.220 --> 01:00:57.340
that didn't really make sense.

01:00:57.900 --> 01:00:59.460
And Rambo was that other game

01:00:59.460 --> 01:01:01.440
I was thinking of much earlier in this episode

01:01:01.440 --> 01:01:03.020
when I was like, Castlevania 2.

01:01:03.440 --> 01:01:04.900
Yep, Rambo, absolutely.

01:01:05.920 --> 01:01:07.600
So, you know, these games exist.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:10.760
Another choice in your experience system is magic, though.

01:01:10.820 --> 01:01:12.920
We haven't talked about magic in depth yet,

01:01:12.920 --> 01:01:15.180
and so I think that should be our very next topic.

01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:17.260
Sean, can you walk us through magic?

01:01:18.500 --> 01:01:23.360
So magic is basically your...

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:29.200
The skills that you acquire via doing side quests

01:01:29.200 --> 01:01:31.480
and going into secret places

01:01:31.480 --> 01:01:35.680
and then getting led by a young woman

01:01:35.680 --> 01:01:37.500
into her grandfather's basement

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:39.280
to be taught these things.

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:43.900
And they can be very useful.

01:01:44.460 --> 01:01:46.020
They can be overpowered.

01:01:47.400 --> 01:01:50.620
They can be incredibly necessary

01:01:50.620 --> 01:01:52.220
just to continue through the game.

01:01:54.200 --> 01:01:57.660
But basically, they're just a skill that you acquire

01:01:57.660 --> 01:02:00.180
and you go to the select screen to start it up.

01:02:00.520 --> 01:02:04.500
And you can turn into a fairy if you want.

01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:05.900
You can jump extra high.

01:02:07.140 --> 01:02:09.480
But the thing that really limits it

01:02:09.480 --> 01:02:12.260
is the amount of magic points that you have.

01:02:12.260 --> 01:02:14.740
Like, you're not really supposed to spam this.

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:20.120
Like, you're supposed to just do regular melee combat

01:02:20.120 --> 01:02:21.080
for most of this game.

01:02:21.080 --> 01:02:23.140
And these are to be used sparingly.

01:02:23.540 --> 01:02:25.340
Yeah, they're used sparingly,

01:02:25.420 --> 01:02:28.160
but they're also not as sparingly

01:02:28.160 --> 01:02:29.660
as I was thinking they were going to be.

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:32.160
Because I think the one nice thing they do

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:34.440
to make it so it's a little less, like,

01:02:35.080 --> 01:02:37.120
punishing to your magic bar to use something

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:39.140
is, like, some things that are helpful.

01:02:39.140 --> 01:02:40.820
Like, there's a shield spell,

01:02:40.840 --> 01:02:42.780
which just makes you more defensive

01:02:42.780 --> 01:02:44.580
and it turns you red, which is kind of cool.

01:02:45.880 --> 01:02:46.880
Once in a while, I'd be like,

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:48.340
oh, why aren't I using that more often?

01:02:48.340 --> 01:02:50.340
Because you use it once on a screen

01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:52.100
and it lasts for the whole screen.

01:02:52.280 --> 01:02:53.680
There's no time limit on it.

01:02:54.400 --> 01:02:55.980
And it costs pretty low.

01:02:56.180 --> 01:02:58.400
Like, I know I can usually use a couple shield spell

01:02:58.400 --> 01:03:00.440
and still have enough to restore my health.

01:03:01.740 --> 01:03:04.000
So I was appreciative that it was, like,

01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:06.760
it gave me some reasons to think about...

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:08.860
Like, a lot of times in a game like this,

01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:11.920
I will just save all my magic for restoring health.

01:03:12.280 --> 01:03:14.560
Because that seems to me to be the most important thing.

01:03:14.740 --> 01:03:16.120
But this gives me reasons to say,

01:03:16.300 --> 01:03:18.260
well, you know, I'm going to have stuff left over

01:03:18.260 --> 01:03:19.300
when I restore my health.

01:03:19.460 --> 01:03:21.020
Not going to be enough to restore my health again,

01:03:21.180 --> 01:03:22.560
so I might as well use other spells.

01:03:22.780 --> 01:03:23.840
So I thought that was nice.

01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:25.960
It's a really cool system

01:03:25.960 --> 01:03:28.380
that feels like a natural enhancement to Link.

01:03:28.540 --> 01:03:31.480
You know, he didn't have magic powers in the first one.

01:03:31.600 --> 01:03:34.380
And I don't think he gets magic powers in many other games.

01:03:34.500 --> 01:03:36.880
I know that occasionally he'll have, like,

01:03:37.260 --> 01:03:38.700
magic abilities or something.

01:03:38.700 --> 01:03:41.280
But this is a full-blown magic system,

01:03:42.020 --> 01:03:44.880
which gives it a chance to, like, reinvent the combat.

01:03:45.040 --> 01:03:48.260
Joe, you were talking about, like, why don't I use shield more often?

01:03:48.260 --> 01:03:49.980
And I think, like, I feel like a lot of people

01:03:49.980 --> 01:03:52.520
make that, you know, connection late in the game.

01:03:52.600 --> 01:03:54.400
Yeah, I did. That they should be using the magic more.

01:03:54.560 --> 01:03:57.880
And that feels Mega Man inspired there.

01:03:57.980 --> 01:04:01.380
You know, like, pressing select, choosing your magic.

01:04:02.180 --> 01:04:05.180
And then, like, you know, even the fact that shield changes your color.

01:04:05.380 --> 01:04:06.920
Kind of like how in Mega Man, like,

01:04:07.020 --> 01:04:10.320
the power-up you choose at that time will change what you look like.

01:04:11.100 --> 01:04:12.880
I thought that that was really cool.

01:04:13.040 --> 01:04:15.160
And it's cool that some of these are missable.

01:04:15.320 --> 01:04:17.380
I know that some of them you need to,

01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:19.400
in order to progress on a high jump.

01:04:19.400 --> 01:04:21.260
Shield. Yeah, okay.

01:04:22.280 --> 01:04:23.680
Yeah, shield is one you can miss.

01:04:23.760 --> 01:04:26.540
You're right. So it's cool that these are missable,

01:04:26.560 --> 01:04:31.940
but I hate that they're given to you by these old men in these basements.

01:04:32.120 --> 01:04:33.360
Not because it's creepy,

01:04:33.420 --> 01:04:38.040
but because this should have been the reward for beating the palaces.

01:04:38.280 --> 01:04:39.140
This should have been the thing.

01:04:39.220 --> 01:04:40.580
And I know that that makes it like,

01:04:40.620 --> 01:04:42.600
wait a minute, Mike, you just said it's cool that they're missable.

01:04:42.740 --> 01:04:46.200
But I think it's a better payoff and a better story for Link

01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:51.160
that he's, like, gaining these powers as he continues to go from palace to palace

01:04:51.160 --> 01:04:54.720
rather than just, like, can you find the...

01:04:54.720 --> 01:04:55.640
What's that?

01:04:56.340 --> 01:05:00.780
The deep lore is that these old men are teaching him this.

01:05:00.880 --> 01:05:02.680
Like, if they're going to be in the palace, like, you know,

01:05:03.360 --> 01:05:04.820
they're all enemies in there.

01:05:04.820 --> 01:05:05.720
Who's going to teach him?

01:05:06.380 --> 01:05:07.960
Okay, so, all right.

01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:09.460
Point refuted there.

01:05:09.600 --> 01:05:12.680
But make the old men, right,

01:05:12.800 --> 01:05:15.140
that you have to track down or whatever,

01:05:15.540 --> 01:05:17.620
make it more interesting.

01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:19.620
And I don't have a fleshed out idea for that

01:05:19.620 --> 01:05:21.780
because you just threw this wrench in my plan.

01:05:21.980 --> 01:05:23.840
But they needed to do something

01:05:23.840 --> 01:05:26.140
rather than just, like, talk to the woman,

01:05:26.660 --> 01:05:28.100
you know, talk to every woman in the town

01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:29.040
until one of them says,

01:05:29.180 --> 01:05:31.680
you should see my father or my grandfather,

01:05:31.680 --> 01:05:32.560
whatever they wind up saying.

01:05:32.680 --> 01:05:34.660
And then you go into their basement

01:05:34.660 --> 01:05:35.520
and he's like,

01:05:35.960 --> 01:05:38.100
yeah, there's really nothing else I'm going to do here

01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:39.640
except for just hand it over to you.

01:05:39.880 --> 01:05:41.940
Like, they just give it to you right away

01:05:41.940 --> 01:05:43.720
and that's it. And I think that that's strange.

01:05:43.720 --> 01:05:46.200
I mean, at least it's different enough from,

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:47.920
because if it's going to be something

01:05:47.920 --> 01:05:51.180
that you just get as a reward in the palaces,

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:53.400
it's just an item that you pick up

01:05:53.400 --> 01:05:59.300
and there's no real extensive cool stuff

01:05:59.300 --> 01:06:00.600
that happens when you pick up an item.

01:06:00.840 --> 01:06:03.780
And they're more or less doing the same things

01:06:03.780 --> 01:06:07.120
except one has a mana bar and the other doesn't.

01:06:09.060 --> 01:06:11.480
And I think that if they were both just like,

01:06:11.600 --> 01:06:12.400
oh, you just find it,

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:16.300
then that doesn't really solve your interesting problem.

01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:20.740
Yeah, I mean, I won't say that it wouldn't be an improvement,

01:06:21.140 --> 01:06:22.480
but I don't think it's a...

01:06:22.480 --> 01:06:25.260
Personally, I don't think it's a flaw the way it is right now,

01:06:26.180 --> 01:06:27.580
especially in this game,

01:06:27.620 --> 01:06:31.080
because we know that when this game tries to do things

01:06:31.080 --> 01:06:34.160
that are like... Like, at least this is something consistent that I can know.

01:06:34.440 --> 01:06:35.480
Oh, if I go in here,

01:06:36.020 --> 01:06:37.920
there's probably some way to talk to this old man.

01:06:37.920 --> 01:06:39.700
If I figure that out, I can get a power-up.

01:06:40.580 --> 01:06:42.360
Whereas this game is so many other things

01:06:42.360 --> 01:06:43.860
where it's like, I would never know that.

01:06:43.860 --> 01:06:44.780
I have to look it up.

01:06:45.000 --> 01:06:48.060
And I fear that if they were to try to go in that direction,

01:06:48.060 --> 01:06:50.900
we would just end up with eight power-ups

01:06:50.900 --> 01:06:53.140
that we have no idea how to get

01:06:53.140 --> 01:06:54.220
until we look up a walkthrough.

01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:58.300
Magic made the combat better, though.

01:06:58.300 --> 01:07:02.500
I think if you didn't have these extra abilities

01:07:02.500 --> 01:07:05.580
and the whole game was just sword and shield fighting,

01:07:05.900 --> 01:07:09.180
I think I would have given up at a certain point

01:07:09.180 --> 01:07:14.060
because of how frustrating the short sword was for me personally.

01:07:14.480 --> 01:07:16.820
Like, there was just a lot of times

01:07:16.820 --> 01:07:19.280
where I thought I was going to hit these enemies

01:07:19.280 --> 01:07:20.360
and it wasn't happening.

01:07:20.400 --> 01:07:23.420
So Magic gave it a chance to spice things up

01:07:23.420 --> 01:07:24.620
every now and again

01:07:24.620 --> 01:07:27.180
and change how I was playing that out.

01:07:27.180 --> 01:07:32.280
And that's something that this game needs more of.

01:07:32.520 --> 01:07:34.820
And I know it's crazy because there's already enough magic,

01:07:34.820 --> 01:07:37.240
but it just needs more variety

01:07:37.240 --> 01:07:39.200
in what it's offering to you.

01:07:39.340 --> 01:07:41.240
This side-scrolling combat

01:07:42.620 --> 01:07:45.300
is what you see in the beginning of the game,

01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:47.280
is what you will get throughout.

01:07:47.320 --> 01:07:50.100
And it's up to you, the player,

01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:52.740
to acquire these extra abilities

01:07:52.740 --> 01:07:56.980
and find the secret other two attacks

01:07:56.980 --> 01:07:58.840
of downstrike and striking upwards.

01:07:59.660 --> 01:08:02.580
That's all on you to continue to change this game.

01:08:02.800 --> 01:08:05.880
The game doesn't push you to do anything else

01:08:05.880 --> 01:08:07.280
but swing your sword.

01:08:07.420 --> 01:08:09.120
And I'm not sure if that's a bad thing or not.

01:08:09.120 --> 01:08:11.260
I kind of wish that I had your revelation

01:08:11.260 --> 01:08:14.480
because I would describe,

01:08:14.680 --> 01:08:18.040
like, once you get past, like, the second palace,

01:08:18.360 --> 01:08:23.400
the frustration meter, like, hits red for me.

01:08:23.520 --> 01:08:25.540
And I think maybe that was

01:08:25.540 --> 01:08:28.320
because I was basically just trying to brute force

01:08:28.320 --> 01:08:29.520
my way through the game

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:31.700
with just sword and shield.

01:08:31.700 --> 01:08:34.820
shield and I was only using my high jump

01:08:34.820 --> 01:08:37.540
when I needed to get up a high ledge

01:08:37.540 --> 01:08:39.200
or something along those lines.

01:08:39.400 --> 01:08:41.680
Like, I just, I wasn't thinking about magic

01:08:41.680 --> 01:08:42.860
when I was playing this game.

01:08:44.580 --> 01:08:47.440
So, yeah, I, it was my fault.

01:08:49.200 --> 01:08:51.800
It was your fault or was it the game's fault

01:08:51.800 --> 01:08:54.320
for not making magic more intuitive?

01:08:54.700 --> 01:08:55.600
Yeah, blaming the game.

01:08:56.020 --> 01:09:25.840
All right. We've touched on a lot of things,

01:09:26.080 --> 01:09:27.820
but we keep saying we're going to talk

01:09:27.820 --> 01:09:28.900
about the palaces later.

01:09:29.180 --> 01:09:30.940
What do you say we just talk about them now?

01:09:31.700 --> 01:09:33.280
I think that's okay, yeah.

01:09:34.240 --> 01:09:35.380
All right, Joe, is that okay with you?

01:09:35.540 --> 01:09:37.580
I guess. Yeah, that's great.

01:09:38.560 --> 01:09:40.800
The palaces are the new dungeons.

01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:43.580
And I don't know.

01:09:43.620 --> 01:09:44.480
I have a lot to say about this.

01:09:44.560 --> 01:09:46.280
You guys mind if I just kind of go on a rant for a second?

01:09:46.520 --> 01:09:47.440
Let's hear it. Tee off.

01:09:47.660 --> 01:09:51.080
All right. It's not, it's not an angry rant of any kind.

01:09:51.080 --> 01:09:53.000
It's just some, some thoughts I have about the palaces.

01:09:53.340 --> 01:09:56.620
This could become a, a very, uh,

01:09:57.100 --> 01:09:59.020
I'm going to say rant because it's winding.

01:09:59.020 --> 01:10:00.520
I don't really know where I'm going with it,

01:10:00.520 --> 01:10:02.180
but it's just observations I had about the palaces

01:10:02.180 --> 01:10:03.360
that I have to get off my chest.

01:10:03.660 --> 01:10:06.120
First off, if the palaces are the new dungeons,

01:10:06.120 --> 01:10:09.820
I don't like it more than I loved the dungeons in Zelda 1.

01:10:09.980 --> 01:10:12.040
The thing that made the dungeons in Zelda 1 cool

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:16.200
is the exploration and secrets that you could find

01:10:16.200 --> 01:10:18.020
within those dungeons.

01:10:18.240 --> 01:10:22.140
In these palaces, there is a lot less exploration to be offered.

01:10:22.140 --> 01:10:26.420
Many times you'll find yourself following a very linear path

01:10:26.420 --> 01:10:29.760
with only one, uh, other room choice.

01:10:29.920 --> 01:10:33.220
You know, it's never, uh, in Zelda 1 because it was top down.

01:10:33.680 --> 01:10:36.200
Most of the time in those games, you're offered at least,

01:10:36.820 --> 01:10:40.160
you know, two or three different paths to branch off to.

01:10:40.360 --> 01:10:44.360
And there's a lot of backtracking involved of like seeing a door

01:10:44.360 --> 01:10:47.860
that needs a key, going throughout the dungeon,

01:10:47.860 --> 01:10:50.320
finding a key and then having the choice to say,

01:10:50.600 --> 01:10:54.280
oh, well, I could use this key back at that other door I found,

01:10:54.300 --> 01:10:58.180
or I could press on and continue to explore other parts of this dungeon

01:10:58.180 --> 01:11:00.660
and bomb the sides of the walls to see if they'll open up

01:11:00.660 --> 01:11:01.800
secret parts of these dungeons.

01:11:02.080 --> 01:11:03.920
That doesn't happen here in the palaces.

01:11:04.080 --> 01:11:06.280
You're pretty much just going in a linear path

01:11:06.280 --> 01:11:07.660
from one room to another.

01:11:09.040 --> 01:11:11.200
And, uh, even if you look at,

01:11:11.420 --> 01:11:14.320
like if you go on strategywiki.com

01:11:14.320 --> 01:11:16.900
and just like look at the entire dungeon maps,

01:11:17.760 --> 01:11:19.540
it's the, the problem there is like,

01:11:19.660 --> 01:11:21.060
yeah, there's like elevators in the,

01:11:21.140 --> 01:11:24.160
in these palaces that offer some sense of choice.

01:11:24.520 --> 01:11:27.760
But realistically, the way that the game designed the dungeons,

01:11:28.080 --> 01:11:31.480
you should stay in order of the path

01:11:31.480 --> 01:11:34.720
and kind of do like first do A, then B, then C,

01:11:34.940 --> 01:11:37.600
then go down and do D, E, and F, you know?

01:11:37.920 --> 01:11:40.520
Uh, there's no like, do A and B,

01:11:40.780 --> 01:11:42.940
go down the dungeon, go to C,

01:11:42.940 --> 01:11:46.620
go back up, do D, go back down, do E,

01:11:46.760 --> 01:11:48.180
go back up, do F, you know?

01:11:48.520 --> 01:11:52.020
Like learning from, um, from what Metroid did,

01:11:52.140 --> 01:11:54.580
where you, where you see the whole map around you

01:11:54.580 --> 01:11:55.560
and what you need to do,

01:11:55.700 --> 01:11:57.940
but it only becomes clear once you get

01:11:57.940 --> 01:12:00.100
your new power-ups what you need to do.

01:12:00.180 --> 01:12:02.060
In this game, they can only be done through keys,

01:12:02.280 --> 01:12:05.280
but the keys are very much, you know,

01:12:05.560 --> 01:12:07.040
doors locked, find key,

01:12:07.500 --> 01:12:10.060
now go back to that locked door you saw and unlock it.

01:12:10.060 --> 01:12:13.440
So, uh, even in the Grand Palace,

01:12:13.740 --> 01:12:16.660
which is the, the last, uh, palace in the game,

01:12:16.880 --> 01:12:19.660
it's a very long winding map,

01:12:20.260 --> 01:12:22.380
but if you look at it, again,

01:12:22.700 --> 01:12:24.680
from a, uh, a strategy wiki

01:12:24.680 --> 01:12:27.020
or from a, you know, top-down point of view,

01:12:27.280 --> 01:12:29.860
if you just move down and to the right

01:12:29.860 --> 01:12:31.420
the entire time,

01:12:31.900 --> 01:12:33.380
down to the right, down to the right,

01:12:33.460 --> 01:12:35.320
down to the right, like, and I say that meaning like

01:12:35.320 --> 01:12:36.520
whenever there's an elevator, go down,

01:12:36.680 --> 01:12:37.920
whenever you can go right, go right,

01:12:37.920 --> 01:12:40.060
you will get to the end of the game

01:12:40.060 --> 01:12:42.080
and there are so many rooms in that Grand Palace

01:12:42.080 --> 01:12:43.660
that ultimately don't do anything.

01:12:44.020 --> 01:12:45.980
They're just there for filler

01:12:45.980 --> 01:12:48.820
or to make it feel grander, I suppose.

01:12:54.560 --> 01:12:57.620
is, is missing in this, in this game

01:12:57.620 --> 01:12:59.620
as opposed to what was going on in Zelda 1,

01:13:00.740 --> 01:13:03.820
but I don't think that it needed to be

01:13:03.820 --> 01:13:05.500
incredibly complicated either

01:13:05.500 --> 01:13:07.940
because think about a game like Deadly Towers,

01:13:08.160 --> 01:13:09.940
one that we are, all hate

01:13:09.940 --> 01:13:11.380
and it's definitely at the bottom of our list,

01:13:11.580 --> 01:13:13.860
that one has crazy combinations

01:13:13.860 --> 01:13:15.500
that are almost nonsensical

01:13:15.500 --> 01:13:17.600
and no, you know, mortal soul

01:13:17.600 --> 01:13:19.280
would ever be able to figure out

01:13:19.280 --> 01:13:21.680
how they could explore all of that

01:13:21.680 --> 01:13:23.820
to get to the other side appropriately.

01:13:23.940 --> 01:13:26.080
I'm not asking for something like that.

01:13:26.080 --> 01:13:30.680
But I'm missing the Indiana Jones style feel

01:13:31.260 --> 01:13:32.840
of the Dungeons of Zelda 1

01:13:32.840 --> 01:13:35.240
where it's kind of like, you know,

01:13:35.560 --> 01:13:37.260
there, there's a, there's a core idea

01:13:37.260 --> 01:13:39.460
presented in the very first dungeon,

01:13:39.680 --> 01:13:41.240
let's say in this one, the palace, right,

01:13:42.020 --> 01:13:43.800
of there's, there's locked doors

01:13:43.800 --> 01:13:45.740
and there's keys and there's rooms to get to

01:13:45.740 --> 01:13:47.540
and there's a map that you can find

01:13:48.060 --> 01:13:51.180
that will, that will show you all the rooms

01:13:51.180 --> 01:13:52.560
and there's a compass that'll show you

01:13:52.560 --> 01:13:55.440
where the boss is. That's all gone in this game

01:13:55.440 --> 01:13:57.880
and instead it's, here's the palace,

01:13:58.380 --> 01:14:01.380
get to the boss, beat the boss, it's over.

01:14:02.280 --> 01:14:03.960
Any, any doors that are locked

01:14:03.960 --> 01:14:06.500
or have, have appropriate keys to be found.

01:14:06.660 --> 01:14:09.240
There's no like finding keys in one dungeon

01:14:09.240 --> 01:14:10.560
and taking them to another.

01:14:10.880 --> 01:14:13.400
I, I, I suppose you could do that in this game

01:14:13.400 --> 01:14:16.300
if you were allowed to skip palaces.

01:14:16.300 --> 01:14:18.020
I, I never tried to go out of order,

01:14:18.200 --> 01:14:21.660
but it was, it felt, it felt like a trick

01:14:21.660 --> 01:14:24.720
in Zelda 1 and here in, in Zelda 2,

01:14:24.720 --> 01:14:27.480
it just feels like you're never presented

01:14:27.480 --> 01:14:31.400
with a, a risk, a risky choice in any palace.

01:14:31.400 --> 01:14:33.020
You're just following along the path.

01:14:33.340 --> 01:14:36.580
Am I crazy? I, I think that the, the deal with the devil

01:14:36.580 --> 01:14:41.400
that you sign when you move from like 2.5D

01:14:41.400 --> 01:14:46.900
or at least top down to going to 2D side scroller,

01:14:47.720 --> 01:14:51.560
is that you're going to have less to work with

01:14:51.560 --> 01:14:55.220
in terms of the layouts of like,

01:14:55.320 --> 01:14:57.560
if you're going to do like a dungeon like this

01:14:57.560 --> 01:15:00.660
with like branching paths or a half-assed attempt

01:15:00.660 --> 01:15:05.500
at branching paths, like it's just going to be clunkier

01:15:05.500 --> 01:15:08.840
because like, while yeah, you can have the equivalent

01:15:08.840 --> 01:15:12.320
of having a room that exits to the north,

01:15:13.780 --> 01:15:16.100
which is just like using an elevator up,

01:15:16.660 --> 01:15:19.700
like each room is going to feel less unique

01:15:19.700 --> 01:15:23.240
because there just isn't as much real estate

01:15:23.240 --> 01:15:25.700
to, to, to make that unique at all.

01:15:25.940 --> 01:15:29.140
And like Metroid was able to do it right

01:15:29.140 --> 01:15:33.120
because it had, it was, it was integrating

01:15:35.140 --> 01:15:39.720
the skills as well as very unique,

01:15:40.860 --> 01:15:43.320
like sprite work in the backgrounds

01:15:43.320 --> 01:15:46.340
and all the things that you were maneuvering around.

01:15:46.960 --> 01:15:51.120
Whereas Zelda 2, it's like,

01:15:51.200 --> 01:15:53.680
they're almost like, I feel a little copy pasted.

01:15:53.800 --> 01:15:55.220
None of the rooms have much personality.

01:15:55.960 --> 01:15:57.520
Yeah, you can move up and down,

01:15:57.700 --> 01:15:59.500
but it's through clunky elevators.

01:15:59.500 --> 01:16:03.420
And it just like the fact that they went to side scrolling

01:16:03.420 --> 01:16:05.500
is what caused this problem.

01:16:05.700 --> 01:16:07.840
I don't, I really don't think that they could have

01:16:07.840 --> 01:16:13.740
made anything as memorable as Legend of Zelda 1

01:16:14.940 --> 01:16:18.080
with the tools that they constrained themselves to.

01:16:19.180 --> 01:16:21.100
Joe, I know you, I know you have thoughts.

01:16:21.200 --> 01:16:24.080
I just want to get one more thing that I remembered here.

01:16:24.220 --> 01:16:27.360
And that is, and Sean, you awoke this thought.

01:16:27.440 --> 01:16:28.320
I didn't have it on paper.

01:16:28.320 --> 01:16:32.200
However, they never went on the platformer side

01:16:32.200 --> 01:16:34.140
for this game, which they should have done

01:16:34.140 --> 01:16:35.800
given its side scrolling nature.

01:16:36.020 --> 01:16:39.080
They never went for the idea that you can do

01:16:39.080 --> 01:16:41.240
Super Mario Brothers style puzzles,

01:16:41.860 --> 01:16:44.020
which aren't puzzles, you know, which aren't puzzles at all,

01:16:44.100 --> 01:16:45.960
but they're fun, strategic, you know,

01:16:46.100 --> 01:16:49.420
jumping and using your powers to get across things.

01:16:49.580 --> 01:16:51.420
They never do that in these palaces.

01:16:51.680 --> 01:16:54.780
It's always just like, all right, well, we'll put a,

01:16:54.780 --> 01:16:56.800
we'll put a, I'm saying a staircase,

01:16:56.800 --> 01:16:59.180
but it's really just blocks of different heights.

01:16:59.500 --> 01:17:00.880
You know, we'll put that here.

01:17:00.880 --> 01:17:03.460
And the player has to figure out how to get through these enemies

01:17:03.460 --> 01:17:06.220
while they have the high ground or the low ground accordingly.

01:17:06.580 --> 01:17:09.220
And that's something that they could have done in these palaces

01:17:09.220 --> 01:17:11.920
to make them stand out a little more that they did not do.

01:17:12.100 --> 01:17:16.280
Continue on, Joe. Yeah, well, I'll speak to that first,

01:17:16.340 --> 01:17:19.380
then just because it's fresh. But my thought on that is, again,

01:17:19.500 --> 01:17:22.260
I feel like that that is definitely an improvement,

01:17:22.520 --> 01:17:25.760
but not necessarily a correction of something that it did wrong.

01:17:28.520 --> 01:17:29.900
Like, now that you said that, I'm like, yeah,

01:17:29.920 --> 01:17:32.600
that would have been awesome. But I never, while playing it, had the thought of like,

01:17:32.680 --> 01:17:34.400
man, I wish I was doing something different.

01:17:37.100 --> 01:17:41.440
And to the point of the exploration, again,

01:17:41.540 --> 01:17:43.860
I totally agree that it is not even close

01:17:43.860 --> 01:17:45.920
to the level of the original Legend of Zelda.

01:17:46.360 --> 01:17:49.200
But I thought they still did a pretty darn good job of it.

01:17:49.360 --> 01:17:53.040
And I think that, like, I did feel like there were times

01:17:53.040 --> 01:17:55.700
where I felt like I was going on branching pathways.

01:17:55.860 --> 01:17:57.980
And maybe because I didn't do what you were saying,

01:17:58.080 --> 01:17:59.260
where I didn't just go from the top

01:17:59.260 --> 01:18:01.600
and like take the top three rooms, bottom three rooms,

01:18:01.760 --> 01:18:02.780
or middle three rooms, bottom three rooms,

01:18:02.780 --> 01:18:04.700
maybe that would have made, maybe it is linear like that.

01:18:04.820 --> 01:18:06.960
And I just didn't notice, but I just kind of explored

01:18:06.960 --> 01:18:08.000
and was like, which direction do I go?

01:18:08.080 --> 01:18:11.560
Where do I find, you know, every dungeon or temple

01:18:11.560 --> 01:18:13.980
or whatever, outside of having the final boss

01:18:13.980 --> 01:18:17.460
also has an important item that you can use

01:18:17.460 --> 01:18:19.200
or sometimes need to use on the overworld.

01:18:19.260 --> 01:18:21.260
So I was looking for those, looking for the boss.

01:18:21.380 --> 01:18:23.040
Sometimes I would find the boss, but then decide

01:18:23.040 --> 01:18:24.720
I'm not going to take him just yet.

01:18:24.720 --> 01:18:27.180
I'm going to go get a little more experience

01:18:27.180 --> 01:18:28.200
while I look for that other thing

01:18:28.200 --> 01:18:29.840
so that I can get that level up at the end.

01:18:30.400 --> 01:18:33.320
And I felt like for being a side scroller,

01:18:33.500 --> 01:18:37.420
they did all right with making it feel like making exploration.

01:18:37.580 --> 01:18:40.820
And I actually really enjoyed the dungeons quite a bit.

01:18:40.920 --> 01:18:42.720
And I'm realizing as we're talking

01:18:42.720 --> 01:18:45.240
that I think a lot of my opinion is colored

01:18:45.240 --> 01:18:48.180
by the sort of unorthodox way

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:50.100
that I used the strategy guides.

01:18:50.840 --> 01:18:53.840
So like maybe it's like a very specific experience to me

01:18:53.840 --> 01:18:55.940
because the way I use the strategy guides,

01:18:56.040 --> 01:18:58.220
I did not read where do I go next?

01:18:58.220 --> 01:18:59.360
Where do I go in the dungeon?

01:18:59.520 --> 01:19:03.640
Because I, not even from a perspective of like,

01:19:03.720 --> 01:19:04.520
oh no, that's cheating.

01:19:04.520 --> 01:19:05.420
I don't want to do that.

01:19:06.720 --> 01:19:08.400
Which sometimes I get that feeling a little bit,

01:19:08.440 --> 01:19:09.980
but it wasn't even that. It was more of just like,

01:19:10.100 --> 01:19:12.720
I hate playing and trying to be immersed in a game

01:19:12.720 --> 01:19:14.980
and having to constantly glance at my guide

01:19:14.980 --> 01:19:16.220
and walk through and feel like I'm following

01:19:16.220 --> 01:19:17.040
a list of instructions

01:19:17.040 --> 01:19:18.360
because then I don't feel like I'm playing the game.

01:19:18.940 --> 01:19:20.420
So what I did with this is I would,

01:19:20.500 --> 01:19:22.120
before a dungeon or before something,

01:19:22.280 --> 01:19:24.160
I would read ahead. I would just kind of scan through,

01:19:24.260 --> 01:19:25.960
be like, all right, where do I go next? So I can find,

01:19:25.960 --> 01:19:27.740
so I could pick out the moments where it's like,

01:19:27.880 --> 01:19:29.340
jump through this invisible wall

01:19:29.340 --> 01:19:30.740
that I would never know otherwise.

01:19:30.980 --> 01:19:32.380
And then I would know, okay, in this dungeon,

01:19:32.520 --> 01:19:33.360
there's an invisible wall,

01:19:33.400 --> 01:19:35.180
but it wasn't giving me the directions.

01:19:35.640 --> 01:19:38.380
So I did still feel like I was discovering everything.

01:19:39.500 --> 01:19:42.020
And, and I just assumed that it wouldn't all be linear.

01:19:42.020 --> 01:19:43.500
So I didn't try that way immediately.

01:19:43.500 --> 01:19:45.380
I just, I just tried exploring

01:19:45.380 --> 01:19:47.260
and I felt like I was exploring.

01:19:47.260 --> 01:19:49.840
I would just say that the palaces,

01:19:49.900 --> 01:19:52.060
the palaces have their moment in the sun.

01:19:52.060 --> 01:19:55.020
And I think that that is strictly based around combat,

01:19:55.020 --> 01:19:56.260
which is something we talked about earlier,

01:19:56.260 --> 01:19:57.880
that this is the,

01:19:57.920 --> 01:19:59.460
this is the chance where like,

01:19:59.720 --> 01:20:02.760
for one, you face a lot more interesting kind of enemies

01:20:02.760 --> 01:20:03.720
in, in the palaces,

01:20:03.720 --> 01:20:06.420
but they're also more one-on-one style,

01:20:06.720 --> 01:20:09.560
you know, showdowns than the random encounters offer.

01:20:09.800 --> 01:20:11.880
So I think like stuff like that,

01:20:11.940 --> 01:20:13.440
and especially the fact that the ground

01:20:13.440 --> 01:20:14.580
is, is a little more dynamic.

01:20:14.580 --> 01:20:15.560
We're talking about high ground,

01:20:15.560 --> 01:20:16.720
low ground, stuff like that.

01:20:17.280 --> 01:20:19.580
Having, having the bridges and, and stuff.

01:20:19.700 --> 01:20:21.200
I think like seeing that stuff in the palaces,

01:20:21.580 --> 01:20:22.960
great. I'm all for that.

01:20:23.640 --> 01:20:26.460
I can't agree with you on the exploration part.

01:20:26.680 --> 01:20:29.060
And I wasn't looking at a guide either

01:20:29.060 --> 01:20:30.200
when I was doing my exploration.

01:20:30.200 --> 01:20:32.600
It was just kind of a notice,

01:20:32.880 --> 01:20:35.400
a noticing thing that I had by the third or fourth palace

01:20:35.400 --> 01:20:36.960
that I was just figuring out.

01:20:37.080 --> 01:20:40.000
Like I haven't had any difficulty figuring out

01:20:40.000 --> 01:20:42.320
like where I should go next or what I should do.

01:20:42.420 --> 01:20:43.920
And I'm not saying difficulty is the answer,

01:20:43.920 --> 01:20:47.060
but I should have to make difficult choices as a player.

01:20:47.260 --> 01:20:49.120
If the focus is on exploration,

01:20:49.120 --> 01:20:50.760
which clearly it wasn't for these palaces.

01:20:50.880 --> 01:20:52.720
The idea of the palaces was,

01:20:53.440 --> 01:20:54.560
you know, to,

01:20:54.800 --> 01:20:57.600
to fight stronger enemies and take down a boss.

01:20:57.600 --> 01:20:59.480
Like that's what they chose to go with.

01:20:59.760 --> 01:21:00.700
I just can't help,

01:21:00.720 --> 01:21:03.840
but notice how, how different that is from the first game.

01:21:04.160 --> 01:21:05.040
Yeah. I mean, I,

01:21:05.040 --> 01:21:05.840
I guess I, I just,

01:21:05.920 --> 01:21:07.140
I did get lost a few times,

01:21:07.260 --> 01:21:08.120
not never too lost,

01:21:08.220 --> 01:21:09.240
never frustratingly lost,

01:21:09.300 --> 01:21:10.260
but I did get, you know,

01:21:10.280 --> 01:21:13.680
I'm down in an area. I fell through random things and fell down and like,

01:21:13.800 --> 01:21:14.700
how do I get back up there?

01:21:14.960 --> 01:21:17.860
And it was minor compared to other games,

01:21:17.880 --> 01:21:18.760
but you know, I, yeah,

01:21:18.800 --> 01:21:19.760
I just had a different experience.

01:21:20.200 --> 01:21:23.160
What do you guys think about like the,

01:21:23.160 --> 01:21:24.200
the lack of,

01:21:24.300 --> 01:21:28.700
not lack of, but the fact that there were less secrets in this game.

01:21:28.700 --> 01:21:33.120
I think we talked about Zelda one being a game that you wouldn't find

01:21:33.120 --> 01:21:34.080
everything out on your own.

01:21:34.200 --> 01:21:35.420
You'd have to like go to the playground,

01:21:35.660 --> 01:21:38.300
talk to other kids. They'd mention something they did and you'd be like,

01:21:38.380 --> 01:21:39.620
Oh, I got to go home and try that.

01:21:39.620 --> 01:21:41.640
But I'm not saying that can't happen here,

01:21:41.780 --> 01:21:45.500
but even things like the heart containers and the magic potions,

01:21:45.660 --> 01:21:50.020
the things that like increase your stats in those regards,

01:21:50.740 --> 01:21:55.300
I felt like there needed to be more secrets like that because those things are,

01:21:55.440 --> 01:21:56.640
both those things are cool.

01:21:56.780 --> 01:21:58.060
Like they're hidden in the world.

01:21:58.120 --> 01:21:59.600
And if you find them great,

01:21:59.800 --> 01:22:01.580
but they don't change the game at all.

01:22:01.660 --> 01:22:06.480
They just are like a satisfying reward for someone who doesn't leave any stone unturned.

01:22:06.900 --> 01:22:08.440
Um, in Zelda one,

01:22:09.060 --> 01:22:10.280
there was a lot of,

01:22:10.440 --> 01:22:12.360
even just like, you know,

01:22:12.440 --> 01:22:14.240
and this is cheating because this is the game,

01:22:14.340 --> 01:22:15.240
the kind of game where like,

01:22:15.320 --> 01:22:20.100
you really didn't have to do much to get to the end and you could take on Ganon being very underpowered,

01:22:20.480 --> 01:22:22.320
but there were a lot of extra things,

01:22:22.420 --> 01:22:23.600
you know, including, um,

01:22:24.680 --> 01:22:25.620
you know, extra, like,

01:22:25.700 --> 01:22:27.940
you know, you had the, you had green link first,

01:22:28.060 --> 01:22:31.220
then red, and then that like light blue colored one.

01:22:31.320 --> 01:22:33.400
It was like, it was cool to go through those changes,

01:22:33.440 --> 01:22:34.400
but they weren't necessary.

01:22:34.680 --> 01:22:35.500
In this game,

01:22:35.680 --> 01:22:38.600
a lot of it feels either necessary or,

01:22:39.140 --> 01:22:45.060
um, it's handed to you in a way that like the game has taught you about this process.

01:22:45.240 --> 01:22:46.740
There's no secret behind it.

01:22:47.240 --> 01:22:49.800
I guess I kind of like a two pronged answer.

01:22:49.800 --> 01:22:53.180
Uh, number one being that if,

01:22:53.360 --> 01:23:09.820
if the only thing on the other side of the door of like the secret is just like a heart container or a pee bag or which is the experience bag or just something kind of pedestrian like that.

01:23:09.980 --> 01:23:11.220
I don't really care.

01:23:11.440 --> 01:23:13.300
Like I, if there's going to be secrets,

01:23:13.300 --> 01:23:14.320
I want them to be unique,

01:23:14.540 --> 01:23:15.900
sort of like the missable,

01:23:16.120 --> 01:23:18.360
uh, missable,

01:23:18.380 --> 01:23:21.960
uh, magic stuff or missable items.

01:23:22.320 --> 01:23:25.760
Like I, I like those kinds of secrets because it's an actual reward.

01:23:25.960 --> 01:23:27.800
Um, and on the other hand,

01:23:28.120 --> 01:23:34.500
I also think that just by virtue of this game being a lot more guided than the last,

01:23:34.800 --> 01:23:37.560
uh, the secrets that are there,

01:23:38.200 --> 01:23:41.660
um, specifically also the more unique ones,

01:23:42.060 --> 01:23:44.080
even though there are the more,

01:23:44.680 --> 01:23:45.520
again, pedestrian,

01:23:45.880 --> 01:23:48.640
uh, secrets sort of scattered around as well.

01:23:48.640 --> 01:23:52.480
Like, it's not as necessary for them to,

01:23:52.480 --> 01:23:55.580
to be so common because like,

01:23:55.620 --> 01:23:59.840
you can't just go right to Ganon like you could in the first one.

01:23:59.960 --> 01:24:03.200
Like you're going to be forced through this,

01:24:03.420 --> 01:24:05.600
like this gauntlet that's going to,

01:24:05.780 --> 01:24:07.440
uh, bring you up to,

01:24:08.060 --> 01:24:10.140
like the power that you need to be.

01:24:10.940 --> 01:24:14.260
Um, so it's sort of both like,

01:24:14.920 --> 01:24:16.740
I don't care about those kinds of secrets.

01:24:16.740 --> 01:24:19.900
And also it wasn't entirely necessary in this one.

01:24:20.920 --> 01:24:23.020
Uh, I don't know where I'm actually landing,

01:24:23.020 --> 01:24:24.740
but that, that, those are my thoughts.

01:24:25.280 --> 01:24:26.120
I like it.

01:24:26.660 --> 01:24:27.600
Joe, what do you got? Well, I,

01:24:27.600 --> 01:24:29.140
I kind of also have like a middle,

01:24:29.280 --> 01:24:30.760
a mid ground opinion on it because,

01:24:31.100 --> 01:24:31.940
um, I,

01:24:31.960 --> 01:24:34.180
I think that those things are talking about the magic that's,

01:24:34.220 --> 01:24:35.080
that's, uh,

01:24:35.340 --> 01:24:38.080
missable. But, and then also there's like something we didn't talk about,

01:24:38.180 --> 01:24:43.220
which is like, there's two regular physical attacks that I know at least one of them is

01:24:43.220 --> 01:24:44.480
missable. The, the up strike.

01:24:44.680 --> 01:24:46.200
I think the down strike might be missable too.

01:24:46.440 --> 01:24:47.920
The down strike is missable as well.

01:24:48.040 --> 01:24:49.600
Yeah. Those felt really cool to me.

01:24:49.720 --> 01:24:52.240
Cause it felt like it wasn't just progress.

01:24:52.240 --> 01:24:55.620
It felt like progress that I made that was unique in the sense that like I

01:24:55.620 --> 01:24:56.880
could have not made that progress.

01:24:57.060 --> 01:24:58.700
I could have totally never been had this ability.

01:24:58.960 --> 01:25:02.480
And now it felt so satisfying to have this like thing that I found that

01:25:02.480 --> 01:25:03.480
like, I knew it was possible.

01:25:03.600 --> 01:25:04.800
I could have missed, uh,

01:25:04.920 --> 01:25:06.200
and the up strike and the down strike,

01:25:06.320 --> 01:25:07.740
both of them were actually really useful.

01:25:08.420 --> 01:25:10.520
Um, they made the combat a lot better.

01:25:10.840 --> 01:25:12.440
There are, there are other secrets.

01:25:12.440 --> 01:25:16.580
Like, you know, you find a lot of heart containers that are just on random tiles.

01:25:16.640 --> 01:25:19.460
Like a random tile will take you to a side scrolling level that has a

01:25:19.460 --> 01:25:21.680
heart container. That feels,

01:25:22.540 --> 01:25:24.280
trust me, I'm always happy to find a heart container,

01:25:24.640 --> 01:25:25.460
but that feels,

01:25:26.600 --> 01:25:28.460
uh, I don't want to say lazy,

01:25:28.680 --> 01:25:30.240
but that feels just like kind of thrown in there.

01:25:30.340 --> 01:25:31.300
It's like, Oh, secret. Here it is.

01:25:31.660 --> 01:25:32.900
Maybe people will happen upon it.

01:25:33.020 --> 01:25:36.140
There's not usually like a hint or like any reason that I feel like

01:25:36.500 --> 01:25:37.520
satisfied in the sense that like,

01:25:37.580 --> 01:25:38.400
Oh, I solved it.

01:25:38.500 --> 01:25:41.060
No, it's just, I, I got lucky.

01:25:41.700 --> 01:25:42.800
Um, like I said,

01:25:42.800 --> 01:25:44.000
happy that I got those heart containers,

01:25:44.120 --> 01:25:45.740
but I, I do feel like some of those secrets,

01:25:45.880 --> 01:25:48.980
like if they're going to be secrets should be something that I,

01:25:49.080 --> 01:25:50.020
that I have to feel like I,

01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:50.960
I earned.

01:25:51.680 --> 01:25:52.760
I understand that.

01:25:52.880 --> 01:25:53.860
Yeah. Yeah,

01:25:54.040 --> 01:26:24.080
totally. We'll be right back.

01:26:25.320 --> 01:26:27.360
Okay, last thing on the palaces.

01:26:27.660 --> 01:26:33.100
Obviously, at the end of everything, there's a dungeon boss.

01:26:33.740 --> 01:26:36.460
So in this game, I guess you'd call them palace bosses.

01:26:36.720 --> 01:26:38.720
But there's one at the end of each one.

01:26:39.460 --> 01:26:41.880
Any particular thoughts on any of them?

01:26:42.160 --> 01:26:47.260
Is it the same fights over and over again, did you feel?

01:26:47.340 --> 01:26:49.400
Or did you feel a nice variety in these boss fights?

01:26:49.520 --> 01:26:52.160
Were these the stellar stick-out moments of the game?

01:26:52.160 --> 01:26:56.000
Or was it just kind of another combat encounter and move on?

01:26:56.320 --> 01:26:59.360
Joe, what did you think? I thought it was a pretty good variety, actually.

01:26:59.440 --> 01:27:01.300
Especially compared to a lot of other games we've gotten.

01:27:01.880 --> 01:27:03.260
I can't remember off the top of my head.

01:27:03.260 --> 01:27:06.500
There were definitely at least one, maybe two, that I was like,

01:27:06.540 --> 01:27:08.140
okay, this is a little frustrating.

01:27:08.360 --> 01:27:10.880
But for the most part, I was pretty happy with them.

01:27:11.060 --> 01:27:14.860
I mean, there's one guy that's kind of like a common enemy you fight all the time,

01:27:14.920 --> 01:27:16.220
but he's riding on a horse at first.

01:27:16.260 --> 01:27:17.220
You've got to knock him off his horse.

01:27:17.220 --> 01:27:20.200
And even though he's a common enemy, he's one of the harder common enemies.

01:27:20.380 --> 01:27:22.380
So that was interesting.

01:27:22.780 --> 01:27:26.260
Then there's like, I mean, a little later in the game, there's like a dragon.

01:27:28.600 --> 01:27:31.680
Each enemy has a different way of approaching for the most part.

01:27:31.780 --> 01:27:36.100
There's some that you have to use specific spells to take down.

01:27:36.300 --> 01:27:38.520
And I was happy with the bosses.

01:27:39.200 --> 01:27:41.040
Yeah, and they're a lot stronger, too.

01:27:41.280 --> 01:27:48.860
You know, it is definitely, it's a one-on-one fight that feels, none of them felt unfair.

01:27:49.640 --> 01:27:55.860
And you do have to, it's a contest with them because it takes so many strikes to kill these bosses.

01:27:56.160 --> 01:27:59.080
But you have a good amount of health, too, as you progress on.

01:27:59.380 --> 01:28:04.240
So I think, you mentioned the dragon one, and I thought that one was really cool, too.

01:28:04.640 --> 01:28:08.320
I don't know if it would be called a dragon or whatever, but like, whatever it was.

01:28:08.420 --> 01:28:09.540
I thought that was fun.

01:28:09.680 --> 01:28:13.900
And it reminded me of the, you know, there was a great dragon fight in the first one, too.

01:28:14.080 --> 01:28:17.040
So Legend of Zelda just knows what it's doing when it comes to dragons.

01:28:18.900 --> 01:28:21.140
But, Sean, you seem like you have some thoughts as well.

01:28:21.640 --> 01:28:24.880
No, mine was more just on like the aesthetic design of them.

01:28:25.080 --> 01:28:27.700
Like, there were some that I really appreciated.

01:28:27.700 --> 01:28:35.240
Like, the one where it's just like a knight and you keep knocking his head off, I thought was just really funny.

01:28:36.220 --> 01:28:38.000
Or knocking his armor off.

01:28:38.100 --> 01:28:43.240
Like, I kind of liked that little flourish.

01:28:43.940 --> 01:28:49.160
But no, I mean, it's a solid cast of characters that you fight against.

01:28:49.320 --> 01:28:51.720
Yeah. So we're talking bosses here.

01:28:51.720 --> 01:28:59.220
Do you think it's weird that at the end there is actually no Ganon fight and there is no return of Ganon?

01:28:59.320 --> 01:29:08.500
And that's just like he is, he's teased all the time in the game over screen that Ganon has returned.

01:29:08.500 --> 01:29:12.760
But that's only because you died and I guess somebody found your dead body and harvested your blood.

01:29:12.760 --> 01:29:20.940
But, you know, I don't think it's weird only because we've had so many sequels now on the NES that have said, like, screw the conventions.

01:29:21.400 --> 01:29:23.140
We don't want the old boss.

01:29:23.140 --> 01:29:24.120
We want a new boss.

01:29:24.240 --> 01:29:28.440
And to be fair, why would Ganon be in every Zelda game on just the second Zelda game?

01:29:28.540 --> 01:29:30.980
Like, maybe they were going to go different every time.

01:29:31.840 --> 01:29:32.780
You know, I think.

01:29:32.780 --> 01:29:34.440
No conventions have been set yet.

01:29:35.080 --> 01:29:42.280
Yeah, I think I wasn't expecting the whatever the final boss was before Shadow Link.

01:29:42.280 --> 01:29:44.240
Like, I don't even know how to describe that thing.

01:29:44.580 --> 01:29:47.180
It was the worst moth thing.

01:29:47.860 --> 01:29:49.400
Yeah. And I agree with Joe.

01:29:49.500 --> 01:29:53.180
He was the worst. But, you know, like, I don't think I was expecting that.

01:29:53.200 --> 01:29:55.300
It wasn't like a big plot point or anything.

01:29:55.400 --> 01:29:57.180
Right. It was just another palace dungeon thing.

01:29:58.160 --> 01:30:01.240
However, it was cool to then have Shadow Link show up.

01:30:01.300 --> 01:30:03.300
I thought that was like, that's awesome.

01:30:03.320 --> 01:30:10.300
That's like the coolest thing I've seen on this system so far.

01:30:10.300 --> 01:30:16.540
Like, the whole, like, I'm 14 and this is deep, like, equivalent.

01:30:16.920 --> 01:30:18.220
Like, fighting yourself?

01:30:18.720 --> 01:30:19.820
Like, come on.

01:30:19.860 --> 01:30:24.660
That's super cool. And the whole visual aspect of it, too.

01:30:24.800 --> 01:30:26.480
Like, the whole, like, Kairosguro.

01:30:26.820 --> 01:30:28.360
Like, yeah, he's you.

01:30:28.360 --> 01:30:32.780
But, like, the background and him are just, like, matte black.

01:30:32.960 --> 01:30:34.300
It was just a really cool visual.

01:30:35.460 --> 01:30:43.820
Yeah, I agree. There's something so, there's something always to me so satisfying about, like, playing a game where you suddenly fight an enemy that's, like, the same size and, like, general.

01:30:44.080 --> 01:30:45.220
Like, it's just very similar to you.

01:30:45.400 --> 01:30:47.400
Yeah. There's just something very cool about that.

01:30:47.400 --> 01:31:01.440
Yeah, and I actually thought that, especially after fighting that moth thing or whatever it was, and I just knew about Shadow Link just because I was like, God, if this is the one before Shadow Link, like, Shadow Link is going to be awful.

01:31:01.780 --> 01:31:04.700
I actually think Shadow Link was easier than that moth thing.

01:31:06.060 --> 01:31:07.020
I think it depends.

01:31:07.240 --> 01:31:12.440
I think a lot of people do consider Shadow Link to be an incredibly tough NES boss.

01:31:12.600 --> 01:31:15.000
But I would say he's not cheap.

01:31:15.500 --> 01:31:26.940
And it's a really cool cinematic moment, too, of, like, okay, so if this whole game was this samurai thing that we're all talking about, right, and this whole one-on-one sword and shield combat thing.

01:31:26.940 --> 01:31:39.280
Like, to Joe's point about there's something really satisfying when it's, like, the same size as you, when it's you, right, it's you versus you, it's now, like, it's just a skills bout.

01:31:39.280 --> 01:31:52.520
And I think it's impressive that they managed to make a really tough yet beatable boss that is essentially saying, you know, show me everything you've learned and I'm going to use all your tricks on you.

01:31:53.340 --> 01:31:57.880
There is a very common glitch that you can just do by hanging out to the left.

01:31:58.440 --> 01:32:00.320
It's fine. It's fine that it exists.

01:32:00.500 --> 01:32:02.720
Some people just want to see the ending, and I get that.

01:32:03.840 --> 01:32:09.500
But, you know, there's also a reward for, like, beating Shadow Link the normal way.

01:32:10.160 --> 01:32:13.480
I will say I did use that exploit a little bit.

01:32:13.660 --> 01:32:15.600
But I wasn't trying to.

01:32:15.640 --> 01:32:16.700
He kept forcing me over there.

01:32:16.760 --> 01:32:21.500
I was like, all right, I guess I'll hang out over in the easy zone and take it a couple hits out.

01:32:22.700 --> 01:32:24.340
I'm not saying I beat the game regularly.

01:32:24.740 --> 01:32:35.140
Yeah, that AI exploit notwithstanding, like, I think I am still impressed, like, what they did with the behavior of that particular boss.

01:32:35.660 --> 01:32:39.260
Whereas, like, it won't just bum rush you.

01:32:39.440 --> 01:32:42.240
It's not on a loop.

01:32:42.580 --> 01:32:44.540
Like, it's reacting to you.

01:32:44.760 --> 01:32:48.920
Like, if you try and run away, like, it'll just wait for you to come back.

01:32:48.920 --> 01:32:59.840
And, like, that's really unique in what I've seen so far with this kind of game where it's just like, this guy's on a cycle, and I just wait for the next cycle to start.

01:33:00.120 --> 01:33:06.900
But, like, it's actually reactive, and it's an actual, like, AI script running, which I thought was pretty cool.

01:33:06.900 --> 01:33:12.220
And Dark Link would return in Ocarina of Time, a very good battle as well.

01:33:12.460 --> 01:33:13.760
Another very tough one, too.

01:33:14.720 --> 01:33:27.740
I think it's honestly even better because of it being in 3D and using your sword combat to its advantage where he literally is just copying your moves one for one.

01:33:27.840 --> 01:33:33.200
And you have to figure out, like, well, how would I ever attack somebody who's just doing exactly what I do?

01:33:33.200 --> 01:33:35.540
Like, I think that's a neat thing in that game.

01:33:35.720 --> 01:33:36.780
It's a great battle.

01:33:37.720 --> 01:33:43.100
There's a lot of things that we can't touch on, like, as in-depth as we have here.

01:33:43.380 --> 01:33:49.040
But I also just wanted to sort of shout out to the music because it never really shows up again.

01:33:49.040 --> 01:33:52.380
