Okay, yeah. Okay, great.

01:35:09.780 --> 01:35:13.500
A few other questions I have posed for you guys are just general questions here.

01:35:14.480 --> 01:35:17.500
Why don't NES sequels follow the success of their originals?

01:35:17.500 --> 01:35:18.900
We're looking at Super Mario Bros.

01:35:19.040 --> 01:35:22.640
2, Castlevania 2, and Zelda 2 all in the same year, 1988.

01:35:23.280 --> 01:35:24.920
And why is that?

01:35:26.560 --> 01:35:30.160
So you mean, like, just not following all of the same rules?

01:35:30.820 --> 01:35:36.320
Like, why aren't they just the actual, like, you know, Pac-Man, Pac-Man 2, right?

01:35:36.740 --> 01:35:40.740
Would just be Pac-Man with more mazes eating pellets again, right?

01:35:40.840 --> 01:35:45.840
They wouldn't be like, no, this time he's going to space and he's going to do a platforming adventure.

01:35:45.840 --> 01:35:50.000
I mean, that's just, I mean, okay.

01:35:50.280 --> 01:36:05.900
I think if you talk about any artist or artisan in media, the ones that are trying to make something good are not going to try and carbon copy their previous work.

01:36:06.040 --> 01:36:14.360
Like, every director isn't going to try and make, like, the exact same movie with different dialogue unless they're bad.

01:36:15.860 --> 01:36:19.220
So I think that this is just what happens with creation.

01:36:19.500 --> 01:36:29.440
Like, if you're going to bother with making a sequel, I mean, yeah, with the art and commerce of it, like, maybe all your audience wants is more of the same.

01:36:29.440 --> 01:36:33.560
But as a creator, I don't think you'd really want to just do more of the same.

01:36:33.560 --> 01:36:41.760
So I kind of like how they're going with their sequels and not being just iterative but actually making a whole new game.

01:36:42.380 --> 01:36:43.440
Yeah. You know what?

01:36:43.520 --> 01:36:44.480
I changed my mind.

01:36:44.560 --> 01:36:47.420
I'm going to talk about that line right now because it's the perfect time for it.

01:36:48.620 --> 01:37:00.060
Because I totally agree that, like, you could just as easily criticize something that just made another, just made it too similar to the original, didn't try anything new.

01:37:00.500 --> 01:37:13.220
And, but I do get the other side of that too where it's like, I have a debate with a friend of mine a lot about Pokemon and how he thinks the formula is very stale, which I understand.

01:37:13.220 --> 01:37:22.120
But also, I think if you change the formula too much, it's not Pokemon anymore, particularly in, like, a video game where, as opposed to, like, a story.

01:37:22.360 --> 01:37:28.420
Or if you copy all your story beats in a movie, that feels lazy and maybe boring.

01:37:29.200 --> 01:37:42.140
If you change story beats and improve mechanics of a game, like, I understand the people who are coming to a Legend of Zelda 2 because they want to play more of Legend of Zelda 1 and go on another adventure in that style with those rules, with that type of thing.

01:37:42.140 --> 01:37:44.380
I'm just not approaching this game like that.

01:37:44.500 --> 01:37:55.580
But I've definitely, you could definitely, you know, call me on other things that I've been salty about that didn't stick at least a little bit to their formula because now it feels like it's something new.

01:37:55.680 --> 01:37:56.620
It's breaking its own rules.

01:37:56.780 --> 01:37:58.180
It doesn't feel like it's the same world.

01:37:58.340 --> 01:38:02.480
So, like, I get the criticisms to it in that sense.

01:38:02.480 --> 01:38:09.600
But, you know, I think that maybe for someone who is a Legend of Zelda fan, this game didn't walk that line closely enough.

01:38:10.120 --> 01:38:21.540
But I do think that if you go into this game without the expectations of Legend of Zelda and you just look at it for, like, what it is, you'll see a lot more merit to it than, like, if you're constantly comparing it to its past.

01:38:21.540 --> 01:38:40.960
So, I guess that to the roundabout way of, like, answering the question, I feel like that a big reason that these sequels do poorly is because maybe because fans, either fans haven't figured out exactly yet what to expect from a video game sequel or the developers haven't figured out exactly what they should do with a video game sequel or a combination of both.

01:38:41.420 --> 01:38:42.980
Yeah, I think all those things are fair.

01:38:43.220 --> 01:38:45.540
I think it's also important to just point out the timeline.

01:38:45.780 --> 01:38:50.380
You know, 1988, who knows what sequels are supposed to be in video games, right?

01:38:50.560 --> 01:38:58.060
I wouldn't say that in movies, you know, like, the 80s was a big point for trilogies and stuff like that.

01:38:58.080 --> 01:39:06.920
But it didn't mean that the second movie had to just be like, okay, we're doing the first movie again, but we're doing it a little, you know, like, we're just continuing on with the story.

01:39:07.060 --> 01:39:08.920
Like, sometimes a new director came on.

01:39:08.920 --> 01:39:11.280
Sometimes the genre changed, you know?

01:39:12.260 --> 01:39:21.340
Video games didn't yet become the formulaic process that is the Pokemons and the Call of Duties of video games, which is not a bad thing.

01:39:21.440 --> 01:39:26.060
I'm just saying that, like, you know, we didn't yet know that if you like Super Mario Bros.

01:39:26.200 --> 01:39:29.020
1, you should just always get Super Mario Bros.

01:39:29.120 --> 01:39:31.180
1 but with new levels every single time.

01:39:31.260 --> 01:39:33.000
Like, we didn't know that that might happen.

01:39:33.340 --> 01:39:36.980
Instead, we got new attempts, which I think both of you made great points on.

01:39:36.980 --> 01:39:39.320
Right, and if you, sorry, just one last thought on that.

01:39:39.560 --> 01:39:44.100
Like, if you think about, just speaking to what you're saying about, like, video games being kind of young at the point.

01:39:44.380 --> 01:39:55.020
If you think about, like, just logically, most people, I think, when you think about a movie would say, when you make the second movie, make a different story with the same characters or a continuation of it.

01:39:55.340 --> 01:39:56.140
So, like, it's logically...

01:39:57.120 --> 01:40:02.880
Like, not just, like, the most obvious ways, but, like, there are a lot of motifs that transfer over.

01:40:03.700 --> 01:40:12.680
There's, you know, the musical callbacks and visiting the old spot, like, visiting some old places from the previous game.

01:40:12.800 --> 01:40:15.740
It's just, like, the gameplay that has changed entirely.

01:40:15.740 --> 01:40:17.120
Exactly. I'm sorry to cut you off.

01:40:17.460 --> 01:40:27.060
No, it's okay. If you think about it, like, logically, it would follow, or it's feel logical that, like, okay, movies are about story.

01:40:27.380 --> 01:40:29.320
And when you make a sequel, you change the story.

01:40:29.740 --> 01:40:32.080
Video games are largely about gameplay.

01:40:32.080 --> 01:40:38.320
So, when you make the sequel, you make new gameplay because people want something new, which isn't always the case.

01:40:38.320 --> 01:40:49.360
I mean, I think the reason that nowadays sequels do very well, video game sequels do very well, is because they figured out a formula in their first game, and then in their second game, they used that to make it, to perfect it.

01:40:50.080 --> 01:41:01.860
Yeah. Yeah, so I think that, like, that's just, like, the logical conclusion you might draw if you're comparing it to, like, its closest relative in the 80s, which is probably movies, or at least what most people see as video games' closest relative.

01:41:02.380 --> 01:41:04.180
Yeah, I think these are all great explanations.

